h1('1. ENTERPRISE & ENTREPRENEURSHIP')
body('Barriers to entrepreneurship, entrepreneurial qualities, intrapreneurship, and risk-taking.')
sp()

h2('Barriers to Entrepreneurship (March 2024 P12 Q6a)')
body('Q: Explain two barriers to entrepreneurship. [4]')
sp()
body('A: [K] A barrier to entrepreneurship is an obstacle that prevents or discourages an individual from starting a business.')
bullet('Lack of finance: [K] Many entrepreneurs struggle to raise sufficient start-up capital. [App] Banks may be reluctant to lend to new businesses with no track record. [An] Without finance, the entrepreneur cannot buy the resources needed (premises, stock, equipment) and the business idea remains unrealised.')
bullet('Fear of failure/risk: [K] Starting a business involves personal financial risk, especially with unlimited liability. [An] The entrepreneur may lose personal savings or assets if the business fails, and this discourages risk-averse individuals from starting up.')
bullet('Strong competition: [K] Established firms may dominate the market. [An] A new entrant faces difficulty attracting customers away from well-known brands with loyal customer bases, making survival difficult.')
bullet('Lack of skills/experience: [K] Running a business requires a range of skills (finance, marketing, operations). [An] Without these, the entrepreneur may make costly mistakes leading to early failure.')
sp()

h2('Entrepreneurial Qualities (Case Study - Priya\'s Bookshop June 2022 P21)')
body('Q: Explain two qualities needed by an entrepreneur such as Priya. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Risk-taking: An entrepreneur must be willing to invest personal savings and accept the possibility of financial loss. [App] Priya invested her savings into opening the bookshop without a guarantee of success. [An] Without willingness to take risks, the business would never have been started.')
bullet('[K] Determination/resilience: An entrepreneur needs perseverance to overcome setbacks and challenges. [App] Priya continued despite initial low customer numbers. [An] Giving up at the first sign of difficulty would mean the business fails before it has chance to establish itself.')
bullet('[K] Creativity/innovation: The ability to develop new ideas or spot market gaps. [App] Priya identified an opportunity in her local area for a specialist bookshop. [An] This creativity allows the entrepreneur to differentiate from competitors and attract customers.')
sp()

h2('Entrepreneur Role (Case Study - Planting Pots Nov 2022 P23)')
body('Q: Explain the role of an entrepreneur. [3]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] An entrepreneur is someone who takes the financial risk of starting and running a new business.')
bullet('[K] They organise the other factors of production (land, labour, capital) and make key business decisions.')
bullet('[An] The entrepreneur bears the risk of loss but also receives the reward of profit if successful.')
sp()

h2('Intrapreneur Qualities (Nov 2023 P13 Q5a)')
body('Q: Explain two qualities of an intrapreneur. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Creativity/innovation: An intrapreneur generates new ideas for products, services or processes within an existing business. [An] This helps the business stay competitive and respond to changing market conditions without the individual bearing personal financial risk.')
bullet('[K] Self-motivation/initiative: An intrapreneur acts on their own initiative within the organisation rather than waiting to be told. [An] This proactive approach means new opportunities are identified and developed, benefiting the employer through improved products or efficiency.')
bullet('[K] Risk-taking (within limits): Although they do not risk personal finances, intrapreneurs take career risks by championing new untested ideas. [An] This willingness to challenge existing practices drives innovation within established firms.')
sp()

h2('Risk-Taking in Business (Oct 2025 P11 Q5b)')
body('Q: Evaluate the importance of risk-taking in clothing design. [11]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Risk-taking means accepting the possibility of loss in pursuit of potential reward. In clothing design, this means creating innovative designs that may or may not appeal to consumers.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Without risk-taking, a clothing designer would only produce safe, conventional designs. This could lead to the business being seen as boring and losing market share to more innovative competitors. Taking risks with bold new designs can create a USP and attract fashion-conscious customers willing to pay premium prices.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Risk-taking can lead to first-mover advantage - if a new design trend is started by the business, it gains recognition and brand prestige. This builds brand loyalty and long-term revenue.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: However, excessive risk-taking could lead to designs that nobody wants to buy. This means unsold inventory, wasted production costs, and potential cash flow problems. For a small business, one unsuccessful collection could threaten survival.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: Risk-taking is expensive - investing in innovative materials or production methods costs money. If these do not generate sufficient revenue, the business makes a loss.')
bullet('[Ev] JUDGEMENT: Risk-taking is essential in clothing design because fashion is a dynamic, trend-driven industry. However, it should be calculated risk supported by market research. The level of risk appropriate depends on the size of the business (larger firms can absorb failures), the target market (high fashion requires more innovation than basic clothing), and financial reserves available.')
sp()

h2('Public Sector Objectives (March 2024 P12 Q6b)')
body('Q: Evaluate the objectives of a public sector energy supplier. [11]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Public sector organisations are owned and controlled by the government. Their objectives differ from private sector firms.')
bullet('[An] A public sector energy supplier would prioritise providing affordable energy to all citizens rather than maximising profit. [An] This is because the government aims to ensure essential services are accessible, especially to low-income households who might not afford market-rate prices.')
bullet('[An] Another objective would be ensuring reliable supply across the country, including remote rural areas that private firms might find unprofitable to serve. [An] This supports economic development and social equity.')
bullet('[An] Environmental sustainability may also be a key objective - the government can direct the supplier to invest in renewable energy sources even if short-term costs are higher. [An] This serves long-term national interests even if it reduces short-term efficiency.')
bullet('[An] HOWEVER: Without a profit motive, there may be less incentive to control costs or improve efficiency. [An] This could lead to waste and higher costs to taxpayers. The supplier may also face political pressure to keep prices artificially low before elections, creating financial problems.')
bullet('[Ev] JUDGEMENT: The objectives of a public sector energy supplier balance social welfare, universal access, and sustainability against the need for financial viability. The weighting depends on government priorities and economic conditions - in times of energy crisis, affordable supply becomes the dominant objective.')
sp()

