h1('2. BUSINESS STRUCTURE')
body('Sole traders, partnerships, private/public limited companies, co-operatives, franchises.')
sp()

h2('Sole Trader to Partnership Disadvantages (June 2022 P13 Q5a)')
body('Q: Explain two disadvantages to an owner of changing from a sole trader to a partnership. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Loss of control: As a sole trader, the owner makes all decisions independently. [An] In a partnership, decisions must be shared/agreed with partners, meaning the original owner loses autonomy and may disagree with partner decisions that affect the business direction.')
bullet('[K] Shared profits: A sole trader keeps all profits. [An] In a partnership, profits must be divided according to the partnership agreement. Even if the original owner works harder, they may receive less than they would have as a sole trader.')
bullet('[K] Potential for conflict: Partners may disagree on strategy, workload, or financial matters. [An] This can damage the business and personal relationships, and is not a risk faced by a sole trader operating alone.')
sp()

h2('Franchise Agreement Evaluation (June 2022 P13 Q5b)')
body('Q: Evaluate whether a franchise agreement is the best option for starting a new fast-food business. [11]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] A franchise is a legal agreement where the franchisee pays the franchisor for the right to trade using their brand, systems, and support.')
bullet('[An] ADVANTAGES: The franchisee benefits from an established brand name that customers already trust. [App] For fast food, brand recognition is crucial as customers want consistent quality. This reduces the risk of failure compared to starting an independent business.')
bullet('[An] The franchisor provides training, marketing support, and proven operating systems. [An] This is valuable for someone with limited business experience, reducing the learning curve and likelihood of costly mistakes.')
bullet('[An] DISADVANTAGES: However, franchise fees and ongoing royalties significantly reduce profit margins. [An] The franchisee must pay both an initial fee and a percentage of revenue, meaning they keep less profit than an independent owner would.')
bullet('[An] The franchisee has limited freedom - they must follow the franchisor rules on menu, pricing, decor, and suppliers. [An] This means they cannot adapt to local tastes or innovate, which could be frustrating and limit competitiveness in specific locations.')
bullet('[Ev] JUDGEMENT: A franchise is likely the best option for someone new to business with limited experience, as the support and proven model significantly reduce risk. However, for an experienced entrepreneur with strong local market knowledge, an independent business offers more freedom and higher potential profit. It depends on the individual risk tolerance, experience, and available capital.')
sp()

h2('Plc Advantages (Nov 2022 P13 Q7a)')
body('Q: Explain two advantages of being a public limited company (Plc). [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Access to large amounts of capital: A Plc can sell shares to the public on the stock exchange. [An] This enables the business to raise far more finance than a private limited company, funding major expansion, R&D, or acquisitions that would otherwise be impossible.')
bullet('[K] Greater public profile/credibility: Being listed on the stock exchange raises the company profile and credibility with suppliers, customers, and potential employees. [An] This can lead to better credit terms, attract higher quality staff, and increase customer confidence in the brand.')
bullet('[K] Limited liability for shareholders: Shareholders can only lose the amount they invested. [An] This encourages investment as personal assets are protected, making it easier to attract many small investors.')
sp()

h2('Co-operative Disadvantages (Case Study - Great Desks June 2023 P23)')
body('Q: Explain one disadvantage to GD of being a co-operative. [3]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Slow decision-making: In a co-operative, decisions are made democratically (one member, one vote). [App] For GD, this means all members must be consulted on important decisions. [An] This slows down the decision-making process, meaning the business may miss opportunities or be slow to respond to market changes compared to a sole trader or partnership where one or two people decide quickly.')
bullet('[K] Limited finance: Co-operatives cannot sell shares on the stock exchange and rely on member contributions. [An] This limits the capital available for growth and investment compared to a limited company.')
sp()

h2('Co-operative Structure (Case Study - Farm Produce June 2022 P22)')
body('Q: Explain what is meant by a co-operative. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] A co-operative is a business owned and controlled by its members, who each have an equal vote in decision-making (one member, one vote). Profits are shared among members rather than going to external shareholders.')
sp()

h2('Joint Venture (Case Study - Seaside Hotel June 2022 P22)')
body('Q: Define joint venture. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] A joint venture is when two or more businesses agree to work together on a specific project or business activity, sharing the costs, risks, and profits, while remaining separate organisations.')
sp()

