h1('4. BUSINESS OBJECTIVES')
body('SMART objectives, corporate vs marketing objectives, ethics, CSR.')
sp()

h2('SMART Objectives (June 2023 P11 Q6a)')
body('Q: Explain why SMART objectives are important for a business. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] SMART stands for Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Realistic, and Time-bound.')
bullet('[An] Specific: Clear objectives give direction to all employees, ensuring everyone works towards the same goal rather than pulling in different directions.')
bullet('[An] Measurable: Allows the business to assess whether the objective has been achieved, enabling performance review and accountability.')
bullet('[An] Achievable/Realistic: Setting targets that are challenging but possible motivates staff. Unrealistic targets demotivate because employees feel they cannot succeed regardless of effort.')
bullet('[An] Time-bound: Deadlines create urgency and allow progress to be monitored, preventing drift and procrastination.')
sp()

h2('Ethics in Mining HRM (June 2023 P11 Q6b)')
body('Q: Evaluate how ethical considerations affect human resource decisions in a mining company. [11]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Business ethics are moral principles that guide business behaviour and decision-making, distinguishing right from wrong.')
bullet('[An] FOR ethical HRM: Providing safe working conditions in mines protects workers from injury/death. [App] Mining is inherently dangerous (cave-ins, toxic gases, heavy machinery). [An] Ethical treatment means investing in safety equipment, training, and procedures even though this increases costs. This reduces accidents, improves morale, and avoids costly compensation claims and legal action.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Paying fair wages above minimum rates and providing good working conditions helps recruit and retain skilled workers. [An] In mining, experienced workers are essential for safety and productivity. High turnover due to poor conditions is costly and dangerous.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: However, acting ethically increases costs significantly. [An] Safety equipment, training, higher wages, reduced working hours, and environmental protection all reduce short-term profits. In a competitive global market, this may make the mine less price-competitive against rivals in countries with lower standards.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: Shareholders may resist ethical spending that reduces their dividends. [An] This creates conflict between stakeholder groups - workers want safety and fair pay, shareholders want maximum returns.')
bullet('[Ev] JUDGEMENT: Ethical HRM is both morally right and commercially sensible in the long run for a mining company. Poor conditions lead to strikes, legal action, reputational damage, and difficulty recruiting. However, the extent of ethical investment depends on legal requirements, competitive pressures, and the company financial position. A balance must be struck between worker welfare and commercial viability.')
sp()

h2('Ethics in Banking (Nov 2022 P13 Q7b)')
body('Q: Evaluate the importance of ethics for a banking business. [11]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Ethics in banking means acting honestly, transparently, and fairly towards customers, staff, and society.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Trust is fundamental to banking - customers deposit savings and take loans. [An] If a bank acts unethically (mis-selling products, hidden charges, irresponsible lending), customers lose trust and switch to competitors. Rebuilding trust after scandals takes years and costs billions in fines and compensation.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Ethical behaviour helps avoid legal penalties and regulatory sanctions. [An] Banks operate in a heavily regulated environment. Unethical practices (money laundering, fraud, market manipulation) lead to massive fines, criminal prosecution of executives, and potential loss of banking licence.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Attracts ethical investors and customers who increasingly choose institutions aligned with their values. [An] Growing demand for ethical banking and ESG investment means unethical banks lose market share.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: However, strict ethical standards may reduce short-term profit. [An] Refusing to lend to high-risk borrowers (ethical) means missing profitable opportunities. Not investing in controversial but profitable industries limits returns.')
bullet('[Ev] JUDGEMENT: Ethics is critically important in banking because the industry depends on trust, operates under strict regulation, and affects millions of customers financial wellbeing. Short-term profit from unethical behaviour is vastly outweighed by long-term costs of fines, customer loss, and reputational damage. The 2008 financial crisis demonstrated the catastrophic consequences of unethical banking practices for the entire economy.')
sp()

h2('Changing Objectives Affecting Shareholders (March 2023 P12 Q6a)')
body('Q: Explain how changing business objectives might affect shareholders. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Business objectives may change due to market conditions, new leadership, or external pressures (e.g., shifting from profit maximisation to growth or sustainability).')
bullet('[An] If a business shifts from profit maximisation to growth: shareholders may receive lower dividends in the short term as profits are reinvested in expansion. [An] However, successful growth may increase share value in the long term, benefiting shareholders through capital gains.')
bullet('[An] If objectives shift towards CSR/sustainability: increased spending on ethical/environmental initiatives reduces short-term profits and dividends. [An] Some shareholders (especially institutional investors focused on quarterly returns) may sell shares, reducing share price.')
bullet('[An] However, shareholders who take a long-term view may welcome CSR objectives as they reduce risk (of fines, boycotts, reputational damage) and attract ethical consumers, supporting long-term profitability.')
sp()

h2('Triple Bottom Line (Case Study - Delicious Cocoa June 2023 P21)')
body('Q: Explain what is meant by the triple bottom line. [3]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] The triple bottom line measures business success across three dimensions: People (social impact), Planet (environmental impact), and Profit (financial performance).')
bullet('[An] Rather than judging success solely on profit, the triple bottom line encourages businesses to also consider their impact on employees, communities, and the environment. This aligns with CSR principles and stakeholder theory.')
sp()

