h1('5. STAKEHOLDERS')
body('Definitions, conflict, responsibilities, stakeholder impacts on decisions.')
sp()

h2('Stakeholder Definitions')
body('Q: Define the term stakeholder. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] A stakeholder is any individual or group that has an interest in, or is affected by, the activities and decisions of a business. Examples include employees, shareholders, customers, suppliers, the local community, and government.')
sp()

h2('Stakeholder Responsibilities')
body('Q: Explain the responsibilities a business has to its stakeholders. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] To employees: Provide fair wages, safe working conditions, job security, and opportunities for development. [An] Fulfilling these responsibilities improves motivation, reduces turnover, and increases productivity.')
bullet('[K] To customers: Provide safe, quality products at fair prices with honest marketing. [An] Meeting responsibilities to customers builds brand loyalty and repeat business, supporting long-term revenue.')
bullet('[K] To shareholders: Provide a fair return on investment through dividends and share price growth, and transparent reporting. [An] Meeting shareholder expectations ensures continued investment in the business.')
bullet('[K] To the community: Minimise negative externalities (pollution, noise, traffic), provide local employment. [An] Good community relations prevent protests, planning objections, and reputational damage.')
sp()

h2('Stakeholder Conflict')
body('Q: Explain how conflict might arise between different stakeholder groups. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Shareholders want maximum profit/dividends vs Employees want higher wages: [An] Paying higher wages reduces profit available for dividends. Both groups compete for a share of the same revenue, creating inherent conflict.')
bullet('[K] Customers want low prices vs Shareholders want high profit: [An] Cutting prices to attract customers reduces profit margins. The business must balance competitive pricing with shareholder returns.')
bullet('[K] Community wants environmental protection vs Shareholders want low costs: [An] Investing in pollution reduction, waste management, and sustainable practices increases costs and reduces profit, but the community demands responsible behaviour.')
sp()

h2('Mining Company Stakeholders (Exam Question)')
body('Q: Analyse how two stakeholder groups are affected by a mining company expanding its operations. [6]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K/App] Local community: The expansion may create jobs and increase local spending, benefiting local businesses. [An] However, it may also increase noise, dust, traffic, and environmental damage, reducing quality of life for residents and potentially harming property values and health.')
bullet('[K/App] Employees: Expansion creates new jobs and promotion opportunities. [An] However, workers may face increased health risks from expanded mining operations, longer hours during the expansion phase, or pressure to work in newly opened areas before safety measures are fully established.')
bullet('[K/App] Shareholders: Expansion should increase output, revenue, and long-term profit. [An] However, the short-term costs of expansion (machinery, development, recruitment) reduce profits and dividends until the new operations become productive, creating a tension between short-term and long-term returns.')
sp()

h2('Stakeholder Interests Affecting Decisions')
body('Q: Evaluate how stakeholder interests might affect business decision-making. [11]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Stakeholder theory suggests businesses should consider all stakeholder groups when making decisions, not just shareholders.')
bullet('[An] Customer interests affect product decisions: If customers demand ethical/sustainable products, the business must adapt its sourcing and production or risk losing market share. [An] This may increase costs but maintains revenue by meeting demand.')
bullet('[An] Employee interests affect HR decisions: If skilled workers demand flexible working, training, and good conditions, the business must offer these to recruit and retain talent. [An] Ignoring employee needs leads to high turnover, low morale, and reduced productivity.')
bullet('[An] Government/regulatory interests constrain decisions: Legal requirements on minimum wage, health and safety, environmental standards limit choices. [An] Non-compliance leads to fines, closure, or imprisonment of directors.')
bullet('[An] HOWEVER: A business cannot satisfy all stakeholders simultaneously due to conflicting interests. [An] Prioritising environmental stakeholders by investing heavily in sustainability may reduce shareholder returns. The business must balance competing interests.')
bullet('[Ev] JUDGEMENT: All stakeholder groups influence decisions to some extent, but their power varies. In practice, shareholder interests often dominate in Plcs due to the need to maintain share price. However, ignoring other stakeholders creates long-term risks (boycotts, strikes, legal action, loss of social licence to operate). The most successful businesses find ways to create value for multiple stakeholders simultaneously.')
sp()

h2('Stakeholders Affected by Product Removal (Case Study - Farm Produce June 2022 P22)')
body('Q: Analyse how stakeholders might be affected by the removal of a product from the range. [6]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[App] Employees: Workers involved in producing that product may face redundancy or redeployment. [An] This creates uncertainty, reduces morale, and may require retraining. Skilled workers may leave the business entirely.')
bullet('[App] Customers: Those who regularly purchased the product lose access to it. [An] They must find alternatives, possibly from competitors. This may damage customer loyalty and lead to loss of other sales if customers switch their entire shopping to a competitor.')
bullet('[App] Suppliers: Firms supplying raw materials for that product lose a customer. [An] If the farm was a major buyer, this could significantly reduce the supplier revenue and potentially threaten their viability.')
sp()

h2('Stakeholder Identification (Case Study - MX Bikes June 2023 P22)')
body('Q: Identify two stakeholders of MXB and explain their interest. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[App] Customers (motorcycle enthusiasts): They have an interest in product quality, safety, performance, and fair pricing. [An] If MXB produces unreliable or unsafe motorcycles, customers are directly harmed and will switch to competitors.')
bullet('[App] Employees (designers, mechanics, production workers): They have an interest in job security, fair wages, safe working conditions, and career development. [An] If MXB struggles financially or moves production methods, employees livelihoods are directly affected.')
sp()

h2('Sustainability Impact on Stakeholders (Case Study - Fruit Farm March 2023 P22)')
body('Q: Analyse how a commitment to sustainability impacts different stakeholders. [6]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Sustainability means meeting present needs without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs.')
bullet('[App] Customers: May benefit from organic/sustainable products they value and are willing to pay more for. [An] However, prices may increase due to higher production costs of sustainable methods, potentially pricing out budget-conscious customers.')
bullet('[App] Employees: May benefit from safer working conditions (fewer chemicals, better practices). [An] They may also feel more motivated working for a business with positive values, improving morale and retention.')
bullet('[App] Shareholders: Short-term profits may fall due to investment in sustainable practices. [An] However, long-term brand value increases, customer loyalty grows, and the business avoids future regulatory penalties, potentially increasing long-term returns.')
bullet('[App] Local community: Benefits from reduced pollution, protection of local environment, and responsible land use. [An] This improves quality of life and reduces health risks from chemical exposure.')
sp()

# ===========================================================================
# TOPIC 6: SOURCES OF FINANCE
# ===========================================================================
h1('6. SOURCES OF FINANCE')
body('Overdrafts, loans, grants, crowdfunding, sale and leaseback, retained profit, working capital, share issues.')
sp()

h2('Bank Overdraft (Case Study - Great Resources)')
body('Q: Explain one advantage and one disadvantage of a bank overdraft as a source of finance. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] An overdraft allows a business to withdraw more money from its bank account than it currently holds, up to an agreed limit.')
bullet('[An] Advantage - Flexibility: The business only borrows what it needs and only pays interest on the amount actually overdrawn. [App] For GR, this is useful for managing short-term cash flow gaps (e.g., paying suppliers before customer payments arrive). [An] Unlike a loan, there are no fixed monthly repayments, giving flexibility.')
bullet('[An] Disadvantage - High interest rate and can be recalled: Overdraft interest rates are typically higher than loan rates. [An] Also, the bank can reduce or withdraw the overdraft facility at short notice, leaving the business unable to pay its bills. This creates financial uncertainty.')
sp()

h2('Overdraft Definition and Reasons (15-mark set)')
body('Q: Define an overdraft and explain two reasons a business might use one. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] An overdraft is a short-term borrowing facility where the bank allows the business to spend more than is in its current account, up to an agreed limit. Interest is charged only on the amount overdrawn.')
bullet('[An] Reason 1 - Seasonal cash flow gaps: Many businesses have periods when outflows exceed inflows (e.g., buying stock before a busy season). [An] An overdraft covers these temporary shortfalls without committing to a long-term loan.')
bullet('[An] Reason 2 - Unexpected expenses: Emergencies (equipment breakdown, sudden large order requiring immediate materials purchase) need immediate funding. [An] An overdraft provides instant access without the delay of applying for a loan.')
sp()

h2('Sale and Leaseback (15-mark set)')
body('Q: Explain what is meant by sale and leaseback as a source of finance. [3]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Sale and leaseback is when a business sells an asset it owns (typically property) to a finance company and immediately leases it back for regular payments. [An] This releases a large lump sum of capital tied up in the asset while the business continues to use it. However, the business no longer owns the asset and must make ongoing lease payments, which is a long-term cost.')
sp()

h2('Internal Sources of Finance (15-mark set)')
body('Q: Explain two internal sources of finance. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Retained profit: Profit kept in the business after tax and dividends have been paid. [An] It is free (no interest), does not dilute ownership, and is under the business control. However, it is limited by how much profit the business makes and takes time to accumulate.')
bullet('[K] Sale of assets: Selling items the business no longer needs (old machinery, vehicles, surplus property). [An] This raises immediate cash without borrowing. However, it is a one-off source and the business permanently loses those assets, which may be needed in future.')
sp()

h2('Government Grants Benefits (June 2023 P12 Q5a)')
body('Q: Explain two benefits to a business of receiving a government grant. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] A government grant is money given by the government to a business, usually for a specific purpose, that does not need to be repaid.')
bullet('[An] Benefit 1 - Free finance: Unlike a loan, a grant does not need to be repaid and no interest is charged. [An] This reduces financial risk and improves cash flow, as there are no ongoing repayment obligations reducing monthly cash outflows.')
bullet('[An] Benefit 2 - Does not dilute ownership: Unlike issuing shares, receiving a grant means existing owners retain full control and do not share profits. [An] The business benefits from additional finance without giving up any equity or decision-making power.')
sp()

h2('Working Capital Management and Small Retailer Failure (June 2023 P12 Q5b)')
body('Q: Evaluate whether poor working capital management is the main reason small retailers fail. [11]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Working capital = current assets minus current liabilities. It represents the funds available for day-to-day operations.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Poor working capital management means the business cannot pay suppliers, rent, or wages on time. [An] This leads to loss of supplier credit (must pay cash upfront), inability to restock, and ultimately closure. Many profitable businesses fail because they run out of cash despite having good sales.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Small retailers often have thin margins and seasonal demand. [An] Without careful management of receivables, payables, and inventory, temporary cash shortfalls become terminal. Overstocking ties up cash in unsold goods.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: However, other factors may be more important. [An] Strong competition from online retailers and large chains may make the business model unviable regardless of how well working capital is managed. If customers simply do not visit the shop, no amount of cash management prevents failure.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: Poor marketing, wrong location, lack of differentiation, and weak management skills are also major causes of failure. [An] A retailer in the wrong location with the wrong products will fail even with perfect working capital management.')
bullet('[Ev] JUDGEMENT: Poor working capital management is a significant cause of small retailer failure and often the immediate trigger (running out of cash). However, it is usually a symptom of deeper problems (insufficient demand, poor planning, thin margins). The main cause varies by business - for some it is genuinely poor cash management, for others it is unviable market position. Both must be addressed for survival.')
sp()

h2('Working Capital as Finance Source (Case Study - Child Play)')
body('Q: Explain one advantage and one disadvantage of using working capital as a source of finance for expansion. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Using working capital means funding expansion from existing cash/current assets rather than borrowing externally.')
bullet('[An] Advantage - No cost of borrowing: Using available cash avoids interest payments and loan arrangement fees. [An] The business retains full ownership and control without external interference, and there is no debt on the balance sheet.')
bullet('[An] Disadvantage - Reduces liquidity: Using working capital for expansion means less cash available for daily operations. [An] This could prevent the business from paying suppliers on time, buying stock, or covering unexpected expenses, potentially threatening day-to-day survival while pursuing growth.')
sp()

h2('Crowdfunding (Case Study - Cartoon Costumes Nov 2022 P22)')
body('Q: Explain two benefits of crowdfunding as a source of finance for CC. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Crowdfunding raises finance from many individuals, each contributing small amounts, typically via an online platform.')
bullet('[An] Benefit 1 - Market validation: If many people are willing to fund the project, it demonstrates demand exists before full production. [App] For CC, successfully crowdfunding costume designs proves customers want them, reducing risk of producing unwanted products.')
bullet('[An] Benefit 2 - No debt or interest: Reward-based crowdfunding does not require repayment (backers receive the product instead). [An] This means no ongoing financial obligation, unlike a bank loan, preserving cash flow for other business needs.')
bullet('[An] Benefit 3 - Marketing/awareness: The crowdfunding campaign itself generates publicity and builds a customer base before launch. [App] CC gains visibility and early customers who become advocates for the brand.')
sp()

h2('Factors Affecting Finance Choice (Case Study - Planting Pots Nov 2022 P23)')
body('Q: Analyse two factors that influence the choice of source of finance for PP. [6]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] The choice of finance source depends on multiple factors specific to the business situation.')
bullet('[App/An] Purpose and time period: If PP needs short-term finance (e.g., to buy seasonal materials for planting pots), an overdraft is appropriate. If long-term finance is needed (new equipment, factory), a loan or retained profits are more suitable. [An] Matching the finance duration to the asset lifespan avoids paying long-term interest for short-term needs.')
bullet('[App/An] Business type and ownership: As a small business, PP may not be able to issue shares publicly. [An] This limits options to loans, overdrafts, grants, and personal savings. The form of ownership directly determines which sources are legally available.')
bullet('[App/An] Amount needed: Small amounts can be covered by overdraft or retained profit. Large amounts may require a bank loan, multiple sources, or selling assets. [An] Banks may be unwilling to lend large amounts to a small business without significant security/collateral.')
sp()

h2('Sources of Finance for a Shop (Case Study - Great Desks June 2023 P23)')
body('Q: Recommend the most appropriate source of finance for GD to open a new shop. [6]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Opening a new shop is a long-term investment requiring significant capital (lease deposit, fitting out, initial stock).')
bullet('[An] Bank loan: Provides a lump sum with fixed repayments over several years. [An] Appropriate for long-term investment as repayments are spread over time, manageable from shop revenues. However, requires security/collateral and interest increases total cost.')
bullet('[An] Retained profit: If GD has accumulated sufficient reserves, using internal funds avoids interest and debt. [An] However, this reduces the safety net for existing operations and may not provide enough for the full investment.')
bullet('[Ev] Recommendation: A combination is likely best - retained profit for part of the cost (reducing borrowing) and a bank loan for the remainder. This balances the benefit of avoiding excessive debt with the reality that retained profit alone may be insufficient for a significant expansion like opening a new shop.')
sp()

h2('Factors Influencing Finance Choice (June 2024 P11 Q6a)')
body('Q: Explain two factors that influence the choice of source of finance for a farming business. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[App/An] Seasonal nature of farming: Farm income is highly seasonal (harvest periods), but costs occur year-round (animal feed, fertiliser, wages). [An] This means short-term overdraft facilities are essential to bridge gaps between spending and income. Long-term loans suit machinery/land purchase where returns come over many years.')
bullet('[App/An] Asset availability for security: Farms typically own valuable land and buildings that can serve as collateral for loans. [An] This makes bank lending more accessible and at lower interest rates than for businesses without physical assets. However, risking the farm as security means potential loss of livelihood if the business fails.')
sp()

h2('Revenue vs Capital Expenditure (Case Study - Cartoon Costumes Nov 2022 P22)')
body('Q: Distinguish between revenue expenditure and capital expenditure. [3]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Revenue expenditure is spending on day-to-day running costs that are used up within the financial year (e.g., wages, rent, raw materials, electricity). It is recorded in the income statement.')
bullet('[K] Capital expenditure is spending on long-term assets that will be used over several years (e.g., machinery, vehicles, buildings). It is recorded on the balance sheet and depreciated over time.')
sp()

# ===========================================================================
# TOPIC 7: COSTS & BREAK-EVEN
# ===========================================================================
h1('7. COSTS & BREAK-EVEN')
body('Fixed/variable/direct/marginal costs, break-even analysis, margin of safety.')
sp()

h2('Marginal Cost (Short Answers)')
body('Q: Define marginal cost. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Marginal cost is the additional cost of producing one more unit of output. It typically equals the variable cost per unit, as fixed costs do not change with output.')
sp()

h2('Fixed Cost (Short Answers)')
body('Q: Define fixed cost and give two examples. [3]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] A fixed cost is a cost that does not change with the level of output in the short term, regardless of how many units are produced. Examples include rent, insurance, salaries of permanent staff, and loan repayments.')
sp()

h2('Direct Cost (Short Answers)')
body('Q: Define direct cost. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] A direct cost is a cost that can be clearly identified with and attributed to a specific product, service, or department. For example, raw materials used in a specific product or wages of workers on a specific production line.')
sp()

h2('Variable Costs (Case Study - Jake\'s Cakes Nov 2023 P23)')
body('Q: Explain what is meant by variable costs. Give an example for JC. [3]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Variable costs are costs that change in direct proportion to the level of output - they increase as more units are produced and decrease as fewer are produced.')
bullet('[App] For JC (a cake business), examples include flour, sugar, eggs, butter, and packaging materials. The more cakes produced, the more of these ingredients are needed.')
sp()

h2('Reducing Variable Costs (Short Answers)')
body('Q: Explain two ways a business could reduce its variable costs. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[An] Negotiate bulk discounts with suppliers: Buying larger quantities of raw materials at lower unit prices. [An] This directly reduces the variable cost per unit, improving contribution and profit margins on each item sold.')
bullet('[An] Improve efficiency/reduce waste: Better production methods, staff training, or technology that reduces the amount of materials wasted per unit. [An] Less waste means less material purchased per unit of output, lowering variable costs.')
bullet('[An] Switch to cheaper suppliers or substitute materials: Finding lower-cost alternatives that maintain acceptable quality. [An] However, this risks reducing product quality which could damage reputation and sales.')
sp()

h2('Break-Even Analysis Uses (Short Answers)')
body('Q: Explain two uses of break-even analysis for a business. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Break-even analysis shows the level of output/sales at which total revenue equals total costs (neither profit nor loss).')
bullet('[An] Use 1 - Planning and decision-making: Before launching a product, a business can calculate whether expected sales exceed the break-even point. [An] If forecast sales are below break-even, the business knows it will make a loss and can reconsider pricing, costs, or whether to proceed at all.')
bullet('[An] Use 2 - Setting targets: The break-even point provides a minimum sales target for the business to cover its costs. [An] Managers can communicate this to sales teams as a clear target, and the margin of safety shows how much sales can fall before a loss is made, helping assess risk.')
sp()

h2('Margin of Safety (Short Answers)')
body('Q: Explain what is meant by the margin of safety. [3]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] The margin of safety is the difference between the current (or forecast) level of output/sales and the break-even level of output. Formula: Actual output - Break-even output = Margin of Safety.')
bullet('[An] It shows how much sales can fall before the business starts making a loss. A large margin of safety indicates low risk; a small margin means the business is vulnerable to even small drops in demand.')
sp()

h2('Decreasing Break-Even Output (Short Answers)')
body('Q: Explain two ways a business could decrease its break-even level of output. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Break-even = Fixed costs / Contribution per unit. To reduce break-even, either reduce fixed costs or increase contribution per unit.')
bullet('[An] Reduce fixed costs: Negotiate lower rent, cut overhead spending, reduce management salaries. [An] Lower fixed costs means fewer units need to be sold to cover those costs, lowering the break-even point.')
bullet('[An] Increase selling price (if demand is inelastic): A higher price increases contribution per unit (price minus variable cost). [An] Each unit covers more of the fixed costs, meaning fewer units need to be sold to break even. However, this may reduce demand if customers are price-sensitive.')
bullet('[An] Reduce variable costs per unit: Find cheaper materials or improve efficiency. [An] This increases contribution per unit without raising prices, reducing the break-even point while maintaining competitiveness.')
sp()

h2('Break-Even Analysis Benefits (June 2022 P11 Q7a)')
body('Q: Explain two benefits of break-even analysis for a business considering opening a new factory. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[An] Helps assess viability: By calculating the break-even output for the new factory, the business can determine whether forecast demand is sufficient to cover the significant fixed costs (machinery, rent, staff). [An] If break-even output exceeds expected demand, the factory investment should not proceed.')
bullet('[An] Supports the business case for finance: Presenting break-even analysis to a bank or investors demonstrates that the business has carefully considered the financial implications. [An] Showing a realistic margin of safety provides confidence that the investment will generate profit, making it easier to secure funding.')
sp()

h2('Break-Even Limitations (Case Study - Seaside Hotel June 2022 P22)')
body('Q: Explain two limitations of break-even analysis for SH. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[An] Assumes all output is sold: Break-even charts assume everything produced is sold at the same price. [App] For a hotel, room prices vary by season, day of week, and booking method (direct vs travel agent). [An] This makes the simple break-even calculation inaccurate as revenue per unit is not constant.')
bullet('[An] Assumes costs can be neatly separated into fixed and variable: In reality, some costs are semi-variable. [App] Hotel staffing costs partly fixed (permanent staff) and partly variable (agency staff for busy periods). [An] This complicates the calculation and reduces accuracy.')
bullet('[An] Static analysis: Break-even assumes fixed costs and variable costs remain constant. [An] In reality, costs change over time (rent reviews, supplier price changes), meaning the break-even point shifts and the analysis quickly becomes outdated.')
sp()

h2('Break-Even Calculation (Case Study - Pop-up Movies Nov 2022 P23)')
body('Q: Calculate the break-even number of customers for PM. Fixed costs = $2,400, ticket price = $12, variable cost per customer = $4. [3]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[Calc] Contribution per unit = Selling price - Variable cost = $12 - $4 = $8')
bullet('[Calc] Break-even = Fixed costs / Contribution per unit = $2,400 / $8 = 300 customers')
bullet('[An] PM needs at least 300 paying customers to cover all costs. Any customers above this number generate $8 profit each.')
sp()

h2('Break-Even Data Use (Case Study - Pop-up Movies Nov 2022 P23)')
body('Q: Explain how PM could use break-even data to help run the business. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[An] Setting ticket targets: Knowing 300 customers are needed to break even, PM can set marketing and sales targets. [An] If expected attendance is only 250, PM knows it will make a loss and can either reduce costs, increase prices, or boost promotion.')
bullet('[An] Pricing decisions: PM can model different ticket prices to see how break-even changes. [An] If price is raised to $14, contribution becomes $10 and break-even falls to 240 customers. This helps decide optimal pricing.')
bullet('[An] Assessing risk via margin of safety: If PM expects 400 customers, margin of safety is 100. [An] This means attendance can fall by up to 100 before a loss is made, showing relatively low risk for this event.')
sp()

h2('Total Costs Calculation (Case Study - Gold Theme Park Nov 2023 P22)')
body('Q: GT has fixed costs of $840,000. Variable cost per visitor is $6. Calculate total costs if 180,000 visitors attend. [3]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[Calc] Total variable costs = 180,000 x $6 = $1,080,000')
bullet('[Calc] Total costs = Fixed costs + Total variable costs = $840,000 + $1,080,000 = $1,920,000')
sp()

h2('Fixed Costs Calculation (Case Study - Bear Bears Nov 2023 P22)')
body('Q: BB sells teddy bears for $25 each. Variable cost per bear is $10. BB sold 4,000 bears and made a profit of $20,000. Calculate the fixed costs. [3]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[Calc] Total revenue = 4,000 x $25 = $100,000')
bullet('[Calc] Total variable costs = 4,000 x $10 = $40,000')
bullet('[Calc] Profit = Total revenue - Total variable costs - Fixed costs')
bullet('[Calc] $20,000 = $100,000 - $40,000 - Fixed costs')
bullet('[Calc] Fixed costs = $100,000 - $40,000 - $20,000 = $40,000')
sp()

h2('Break-Even for Bicycle Manufacturer (June 2023 P13 Q5b)')
body('Q: Evaluate the usefulness of break-even analysis for a bicycle manufacturer. [11]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Break-even analysis calculates the level of output at which total costs equal total revenue.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Helps the manufacturer decide whether to introduce a new model. [An] By calculating break-even for the new bicycle, management can assess whether forecast demand exceeds the break-even point, supporting go/no-go investment decisions.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Helps set production targets and assess the impact of cost changes. [An] If component costs rise, the new break-even point shows how many more bicycles must be sold, informing pricing or cost-cutting decisions.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: However, a bicycle manufacturer likely produces multiple models at different prices. [An] Break-even analysis works best for single products with one price - for a multi-product business, it oversimplifies by assuming a single average price and cost.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: Assumes all bicycles produced are sold. [An] In reality, some may remain unsold (seasonal demand, fashion changes), meaning actual results differ from break-even predictions.')
bullet('[Ev] JUDGEMENT: Break-even analysis is a useful starting point for decision-making but should not be relied upon alone. For a bicycle manufacturer with multiple products and varying demand, it provides an approximate guide rather than precise prediction. It is most useful when combined with market research, cash flow forecasting, and sensitivity analysis.')
sp()

h2('Total Cost Calculation (Case Study - Benjamin\'s Beds March 2022 P21)')
body('Q: BB produces 500 beds per month. Fixed costs are $40,000. Variable cost per bed is $120. Calculate total cost per month. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[Calc] Total variable costs = 500 x $120 = $60,000')
bullet('[Calc] Total costs = Fixed costs + Total variable costs = $40,000 + $60,000 = $100,000')
sp()

h2('Profit Calculation (Case Study - Farm Produce June 2022 P22)')
body('Q: FP sells 10,000 boxes at $8 each. Variable cost per box is $3. Fixed costs are $30,000. Calculate profit. [3]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[Calc] Total revenue = 10,000 x $8 = $80,000')
bullet('[Calc] Total variable costs = 10,000 x $3 = $30,000')
bullet('[Calc] Profit = Revenue - Variable costs - Fixed costs = $80,000 - $30,000 - $30,000 = $20,000')
sp()

h2('Margin of Safety Calculation (Case Study - Seaside Hotel June 2022 P22)')
body('Q: SH break-even is 5,000 room nights. Current sales are 7,000 room nights. Calculate the margin of safety. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[Calc] Margin of safety = Current output - Break-even output = 7,000 - 5,000 = 2,000 room nights')
bullet('[An] This means SH can afford a fall of up to 2,000 room nights (about 29%) before making a loss.')
sp()

h2('Profit Margin Calculation (Case Study - Move Well Nov 2022 P21)')
body('Q: MW has revenue of $200,000 and profit of $30,000. Calculate the profit margin. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[Calc] Profit margin = (Profit / Revenue) x 100 = ($30,000 / $200,000) x 100 = 15%')
bullet('[An] This means MW keeps 15 cents as profit from every dollar of sales revenue.')
sp()

h2('Closing Balance Calculation (Case Study - Office Furniture Designs June 2023 P22)')
body('Q: OFD has an opening balance of $3,000, total inflows of $18,000, and total outflows of $19,500. Calculate the closing balance. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[Calc] Net cash flow = Total inflows - Total outflows = $18,000 - $19,500 = -$1,500')
bullet('[Calc] Closing balance = Opening balance + Net cash flow = $3,000 + (-$1,500) = $1,500')
sp()

h2('Cost Information for Restaurant Success (March 2023 P12 Q5b)')
body('Q: Evaluate the importance of accurate cost information for the success of a new restaurant. [11]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Cost information includes knowledge of fixed costs (rent, salaries, insurance) and variable costs (ingredients, energy per meal served).')
bullet('[An] FOR: Accurate cost information enables correct menu pricing. [App] A restaurant must price dishes above their cost to make profit. [An] Without knowing the exact ingredient and labour cost per dish, prices may be set too low (making losses) or too high (losing customers to competitors).')
bullet('[An] FOR: Enables break-even analysis. [An] The owner can calculate how many covers (meals) must be served daily/weekly to cover costs, setting realistic targets for staff and informing marketing spend decisions.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Controls waste and inefficiency. [An] By tracking costs per dish, the restaurant can identify where waste occurs (e.g., expensive ingredients spoiling), take corrective action, and improve profitability.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: However, cost information alone does not guarantee success. [An] A restaurant could have perfect cost control but fail because of poor location, bad food quality, weak marketing, or strong competition. Demand-side factors are equally or more important.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: Costs constantly change (ingredient prices, energy costs, wage rates), so cost information quickly becomes outdated. [An] The business must continuously update its information, which is time-consuming for a small restaurant owner already managing multiple roles.')
bullet('[Ev] JUDGEMENT: Accurate cost information is essential but not sufficient for restaurant success. It enables profitable pricing and cost control, which are necessary conditions for survival. However, success also requires quality food, good service, effective marketing, and suitable location. Cost information is a foundation upon which other success factors are built.')
sp()

h2('Accurate Cost Information Uses (Short Answers)')
body('Q: Explain two uses of accurate cost information for a business. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[An] Pricing decisions: Accurate cost data ensures prices are set above cost to generate profit. [An] Without this, a business may unknowingly sell products at a loss, particularly if variable costs have increased without being tracked.')
bullet('[An] Budgeting and forecasting: Accurate costs enable realistic budgets and cash flow forecasts. [An] This helps secure finance (banks require reliable projections), set spending limits for departments, and identify variances early so corrective action can be taken.')
sp()

h2('Cost Information Evaluation (Case Study - Plasshape Nov 2023 P21)')
body('Q: Explain how PS could use cost information when making decisions. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[App/An] Setting prices for plastic products: Knowing the exact material, energy, and labour cost per product ensures PS sets prices that cover costs and generate profit. [An] For different products (varying complexity/size), different costs apply, so each must be individually priced based on accurate cost data.')
bullet('[App/An] Deciding whether to accept special orders: If a customer offers a lower price for a bulk order, PS can assess whether the price exceeds variable cost (making a positive contribution). [An] This contribution costing approach means PS can profitably accept orders above variable cost even if below normal price, as long as there is spare capacity.')
