# TOPIC 8: CASH FLOW FORECASTING
# ===========================================================================
h1('8. CASH FLOW FORECASTING')
body('Forecasts, budgets, improving cash flow.')
sp()

h2('Cash Flow Forecast Benefits (Nov 2022 P11 Q7a)')
body('Q: Explain two benefits of preparing a cash flow forecast. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] A cash flow forecast is a prediction of all expected money coming into and going out of a business over a future period.')
bullet('[An] Benefit 1 - Identifies potential shortfalls in advance: By forecasting when outflows will exceed inflows, the business can arrange finance (overdraft, loan) before the cash shortage occurs. [An] Without this foresight, the business might suddenly be unable to pay suppliers or staff, causing a crisis.')
bullet('[An] Benefit 2 - Supports applications for finance: Banks and investors require cash flow forecasts before lending. [An] A well-prepared forecast demonstrates that the business has planned carefully, understands its financial needs, and can afford repayments, increasing the chance of securing funding on favourable terms.')
sp()

h2('Cash Flow Forecast Purposes (June 2023 P13 Q5a)')
body('Q: Explain two purposes of a cash flow forecast for a new business. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[An] Purpose 1 - Planning: Shows the entrepreneur how much start-up capital is needed and when additional finance will be required. [An] This prevents the common mistake of underestimating cash needs, which causes many new businesses to fail within the first year.')
bullet('[An] Purpose 2 - Monitoring and control: Once trading begins, actual cash flows can be compared to the forecast. [An] Significant differences highlight problems (lower-than-expected sales, higher costs) early, allowing corrective action before the situation becomes critical.')
sp()

h2('Cash vs Profit (Case Study - Priya\'s Bookshop June 2022 P21)')
body('Q: Explain the difference between cash and profit. [3]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Cash is the actual money available to the business at any point in time - money in the bank account and in hand.')
bullet('[K] Profit is the surplus of total revenue over total costs over a period (revenue minus all expenses).')
bullet('[An] A business can be profitable but have no cash (e.g., if customers owe money/trade receivables, or if profit has been spent on assets). Conversely, a loss-making business may temporarily have cash (from a loan or selling assets). Cash pays bills; profit is an accounting concept.')
sp()

h2('Cash Flow Forecast Calculation (Case Study - Priya\'s Bookshop June 2022 P21)')
body('Q: PB has opening balance $2,000, cash inflows $8,500, cash outflows $9,200. Calculate the closing balance. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[Calc] Net cash flow = $8,500 - $9,200 = -$700')
bullet('[Calc] Closing balance = $2,000 + (-$700) = $1,300')
sp()

h2('Benefits of Cash Flow Forecast (Case Study - Priya\'s Bookshop June 2022 P21)')
body('Q: Explain two benefits of a cash flow forecast for PB. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[An] Identifies when overdraft/finance will be needed: If PB forecasts a negative closing balance in certain months (e.g., after buying new stock), Priya can arrange overdraft facilities in advance. [An] This prevents the embarrassment and damage of bounced payments or inability to restock.')
bullet('[An] Helps plan stock purchases: By seeing when cash is available, Priya can time bulk book purchases for months with positive cash flow. [An] This avoids paying interest on borrowed money for stock purchases and ensures the shop remains well-stocked during peak periods.')
sp()

h2('Cash Flow Forecast Calculation (Case Study - Great Desks June 2023 P23)')
body('Q: GD has opening balance $5,500, total inflows $12,000, wages $4,000, materials $6,500, other costs $2,800. Calculate closing balance. [3]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[Calc] Total outflows = $4,000 + $6,500 + $2,800 = $13,300')
bullet('[Calc] Net cash flow = $12,000 - $13,300 = -$1,300')
bullet('[Calc] Closing balance = $5,500 + (-$1,300) = $4,200')
sp()

h2('Closing Balance Calculation (Case Study - Jake\'s Cakes Nov 2023 P23)')
body('Q: JC opening balance = $800. Inflows = $3,200. Outflows: ingredients $1,400, rent $600, wages $900, other $450. Calculate closing balance. [3]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[Calc] Total outflows = $1,400 + $600 + $900 + $450 = $3,350')
bullet('[Calc] Net cash flow = $3,200 - $3,350 = -$150')
bullet('[Calc] Closing balance = $800 + (-$150) = $650')
sp()

h2('Improving Cash Flow (Short Answers)')
body('Q: Explain three methods a business could use to improve its cash flow. [6]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[An] Reduce trade receivables period: Offer early payment discounts or tighten credit terms to get customers to pay faster. [An] This brings cash into the business sooner, reducing the gap between spending on production and receiving payment. However, tighter terms may lose some customers.')
bullet('[An] Delay payments to suppliers: Negotiate longer payment terms (e.g., 60 days instead of 30). [An] This keeps cash in the business longer, improving the cash position. However, this may damage supplier relationships or lose early payment discounts.')
bullet('[An] Reduce inventory levels: Hold less stock to free up cash tied up in unsold goods. [An] Adopting JIT reduces the cash invested in inventory. However, this risks stockouts if demand is unexpectedly high or deliveries are delayed.')
bullet('[An] Sale and leaseback: Sell owned assets and lease them back. [An] This immediately releases a large lump sum while continuing to use the asset. However, the business loses ownership and faces ongoing lease costs.')
sp()

h2('Improving Cash Flow (Case Study - Move Well Nov 2022 P21)')
body('Q: Explain two ways MW could improve its cash flow. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[App/An] Require upfront payment or deposits from customers: Instead of allowing credit, MW could require full or partial payment when services are booked. [An] This brings cash in earlier, reducing the gap between delivering services and receiving payment, preventing cash shortfalls.')
bullet('[App/An] Negotiate better terms with suppliers: MW could negotiate longer payment periods for equipment or supplies. [An] This delays cash going out while still receiving goods/services on time, improving the net cash position each month.')
sp()

h2('Budgets for Farming Business (June 2024 P11 Q6b)')
body('Q: Evaluate the usefulness of budgets for a farming business. [11]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] A budget is a financial plan for a future period, setting out expected income and expenditure, used for planning, coordination, and control.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Budgets help farmers plan for seasonal fluctuations. [App] Farming has very seasonal income (harvest) but year-round costs (animal feed, fertiliser, wages). [An] A budget shows when cash will be tight, allowing the farmer to arrange finance in advance rather than facing unexpected shortfalls.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Enables cost control by setting spending limits for different activities (fertiliser, seed, labour). [An] Comparing actual spending to budget identifies overspending early, allowing corrective action before financial problems develop.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Supports loan applications. [An] Banks require evidence of financial planning before lending for expensive farming equipment or land. A detailed budget demonstrates the farmer understands their finances and can afford repayments.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: However, farming is highly unpredictable. [An] Weather, disease, market prices, and government policy changes can make budgets inaccurate quickly. A drought could halve yields, making an optimistic budget meaningless and potentially misleading.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: Budgets are time-consuming to prepare and maintain. [An] A farmer already managing multiple activities (crops, livestock, machinery) may lack time for detailed financial planning, and the cost of professional accountancy advice may be prohibitive for a small farm.')
bullet('[Ev] JUDGEMENT: Budgets are useful for farming businesses as a planning framework and for securing finance. However, they must be flexible enough to accommodate the inherent unpredictability of agriculture. Zero-based or flexible budgeting approaches may be more appropriate than rigid incremental budgets. The key is using budgets as guides rather than fixed plans.')
sp()

h2('Cash Flow Forecasting for New Restaurant (March 2023 P12 Q5)')
body('Q: Evaluate the importance of cash flow forecasting for a new restaurant. [11]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] A cash flow forecast predicts money flowing in and out over future months.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Essential for securing start-up finance. [App] A new restaurant needs significant investment (equipment, fitting out, initial stock, deposits) before any revenue is generated. [An] Banks will not lend without seeing a cash flow forecast showing how and when the loan will be repaid.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Identifies the critical early months when outflows exceed inflows. [An] Most restaurants take months to build a customer base. The forecast shows how much working capital is needed to survive this period, preventing the owner from underestimating start-up funding needs.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Helps plan seasonal patterns. [An] Restaurants often experience quiet periods (January, weekdays). Forecasting these allows advance planning for reduced staff hours or promotional activities.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: However, forecasts for new businesses are inherently unreliable because there is no trading history. [An] Estimates of customer numbers, average spend, and food costs are guesses based on limited information. The forecast may give false confidence if assumptions are overly optimistic.')
bullet('[Ev] JUDGEMENT: Cash flow forecasting is critically important for a new restaurant - it is probably the single most important financial planning tool at start-up. However, it must be based on realistic, conservative assumptions, updated frequently as actual data becomes available, and treated as a living document rather than a one-off exercise.')
sp()

