h2('Market Share Calculation (Case Study - Great Resources)')
body('Q: GR has sales of $2m in a market worth $25m. Calculate GR market share. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[Calc] Market share = (Business sales / Total market sales) x 100 = ($2m / $25m) x 100 = 8%')
sp()

h2('Market Share Calculation (Case Study - MX Bikes June 2023 P22)')
body('Q: MXB sells 800 motorcycles. Total market sales are 5,000 motorcycles. Calculate market share. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[Calc] Market share = (800 / 5,000) x 100 = 16%')
sp()

h2('Market Share Calculation (Case Study - Planting Pots Nov 2022 P23)')
body('Q: PP has revenue of $120,000. The market is worth $2m. Calculate market share. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[Calc] Market share = ($120,000 / $2,000,000) x 100 = 6%')
sp()

h2('Market Share Fall Implication (Case Study - Planting Pots Nov 2022 P23)')
body('Q: Explain one implication for PP of a fall in market share. [3]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[An] A falling market share means PP is losing competitiveness relative to rivals - customers are choosing competitor products instead. [An] This could lead to lower revenue, reduced economies of scale (higher unit costs), and a weakening brand position. If the trend continues, PP may become unviable as it cannot cover fixed costs with declining sales.')
sp()

h2('Market Growth Calculation (Case Study - Custom Motorcycles Nov 2023 P23)')
body('Q: The motorcycle market was worth $50m last year and $55m this year. Calculate market growth. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[Calc] Market growth = ((New value - Old value) / Old value) x 100 = (($55m - $50m) / $50m) x 100 = 10%')
sp()

h2('Increasing Market Share (20-mark set)')
body('Q: Explain two ways a business could increase its market share. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[An] Competitive pricing: Reducing prices below competitors attracts price-sensitive customers who switch brands. [An] In a price-elastic market, the increase in volume more than compensates for lower margins, growing market share. However, competitors may match price cuts, starting a price war.')
bullet('[An] Product improvement/innovation: Developing superior products with unique features or better quality that attract customers from rivals. [An] This creates competitive advantage without sacrificing margins - customers pay full price for a better product. However, R&D is expensive and innovation may not succeed.')
sp()

h2('Supply Definition (Case Study - Child Play)')
body('Q: Define supply. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Supply is the quantity of a good or service that producers are willing and able to offer for sale at a given price in a given time period. Generally, as price increases, quantity supplied increases (law of supply).')
sp()

h2('Demand Definition (20-mark set)')
body('Q: Define demand. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Demand is the quantity of a good or service that consumers are willing and able to buy at a given price in a given time period. Generally, as price falls, quantity demanded increases (law of demand).')
sp()

h2('Supply Factors (20-mark set)')
body('Q: Explain two factors that affect supply. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[An] Cost of production: If input costs (raw materials, wages, energy) rise, supplying becomes less profitable at the current price. [An] Some producers may reduce output or exit the market, reducing supply. Conversely, falling costs increase profitability and encourage greater supply.')
bullet('[An] Technology: New technology can make production more efficient, reducing costs per unit. [An] This makes it profitable to produce more at any given price, increasing supply. For example, automation in manufacturing reduces labour costs and increases output capacity.')
sp()

h2('Factors Affecting Supply (Case Study - Farm Produce June 2022 P22)')
body('Q: Explain two factors that could affect the supply of farm produce. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[App/An] Weather conditions: Good weather increases crop yields and therefore supply. [An] Poor weather (drought, frost, flooding) destroys crops, dramatically reducing supply regardless of market price. This makes agricultural supply particularly unpredictable.')
bullet('[App/An] Input costs: Rising fertiliser, seed, fuel, or labour costs make farming less profitable. [An] If costs rise above the price received, farmers may switch to other crops or reduce planted area, decreasing supply of that product.')
sp()

h2('Market Segmentation (Case Study - Priya\'s Bookshop June 2022 P21)')
body('Q: Explain what is meant by market segmentation. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Market segmentation is the process of dividing a market into distinct groups of consumers who have similar needs, characteristics, or behaviours, so that different marketing strategies can be targeted at each group. Bases include demographics (age, income, gender), geography, psychographics (lifestyle, values), and behaviour (usage rate, loyalty).')
sp()

h2('Market Segmentation Benefits (20-mark set)')
body('Q: Explain two benefits of market segmentation for a business. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[An] More targeted marketing: Instead of a generic campaign, the business can tailor its marketing mix to each segment. [An] This increases effectiveness because messages resonate with specific customer needs, improving conversion rates and reducing wasted marketing spend on uninterested consumers.')
bullet('[An] Better product development: Understanding different segment needs helps develop products that precisely match what customers want. [An] This increases customer satisfaction and willingness to pay, potentially allowing premium pricing for products that closely match segment needs.')
sp()

h2('Market Segmentation Benefits (Nov 2022 P12 Q5a)')
body('Q: Explain two benefits of market segmentation for a motor car manufacturer. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[App/An] Targeted product development: Segmenting by income/lifestyle allows the manufacturer to produce different models for different segments (economy cars for budget buyers, luxury for wealthy buyers, SUVs for families). [An] Each model precisely meets its target segment needs, maximising sales across the entire market rather than trying to serve everyone with one product.')
bullet('[App/An] Effective promotion: Different segments respond to different messages and media. [An] Young buyers might be reached via social media and digital advertising; luxury buyers through premium magazines and exclusive events. This targeted approach is more cost-effective than mass advertising and generates better response rates.')
sp()

h2('Marketing Adding Value (Nov 2022 P11 Q5a)')
body('Q: Explain two ways marketing can add value to a product. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[An] Branding: Creating a strong brand identity makes customers perceive the product as superior to unbranded alternatives. [An] Customers willingly pay a premium for branded products (e.g., Nike vs generic trainers), even if the physical product is similar. The brand adds value by creating emotional connection, status, and trust.')
bullet('[An] Packaging: Attractive, innovative, or functional packaging makes the product more appealing and convenient. [An] Premium packaging (e.g., Apple product packaging) creates an experience that justifies higher prices. It differentiates from competitors and can communicate quality before the product is even used.')
sp()

h2('Marketing Objectives with Other Departments (Nov 2022 P11 Q5b)')
body('Q: Evaluate how marketing objectives might conflict with other departments in a hotel. [11]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Marketing objectives might include increasing occupancy rates, attracting new market segments, or increasing average room rates.')
bullet('[An] Conflict with Finance: Marketing may want large promotional budgets to attract guests, but finance wants to control costs and maintain profit margins. [An] An expensive advertising campaign reduces short-term profit even if it builds long-term brand awareness. Finance may reject marketing budget requests, limiting promotional activity.')
bullet('[An] Conflict with Operations/HRM: Marketing promises (luxury service, personalised attention) must be delivered by operational staff. [An] If marketing promotes services the hotel cannot consistently deliver (due to understaffing or insufficient training), customer disappointment damages reputation more than no promotion at all.')
bullet('[An] Conflict with Finance on pricing: Marketing may want promotional room rates to fill empty rooms, but finance needs rates that cover costs and generate profit. [An] Heavily discounted rooms may fill the hotel but at unsustainably low margins, or may attract price-sensitive guests who spend little on premium services.')
bullet('[An] HOWEVER: Objectives should be aligned through corporate objectives. [An] If the corporate objective is growth, all departments should support marketing expansion. If it is profitability, marketing should focus on premium positioning rather than volume discounting.')
bullet('[Ev] JUDGEMENT: Some conflict between marketing and other departments is inevitable because they have different functional priorities. The key is managing this through clear corporate objectives that provide an overall direction, regular cross-functional meetings, and compromise. Marketing should not operate in isolation but should integrate with operations, finance, and HRM to make promises that can be profitably delivered.')
sp()

h2('Corporate vs Marketing Objectives Link (20-mark set)')
body('Q: Explain the link between corporate objectives and marketing objectives. [3]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Corporate objectives are the overall goals of the entire business (e.g., growth, profit, market share). Marketing objectives are the goals set specifically for the marketing function (e.g., increase market share by 5%, launch 2 new products).')
bullet('[An] Marketing objectives should be derived from and support corporate objectives. For example, if the corporate objective is to grow revenue by 20%, marketing might set objectives to increase market share, enter new segments, or launch new products to achieve that growth. Marketing is a tool for delivering corporate goals.')
sp()

h2('Market Research Definition (10-mark set)')
body('Q: Define market research. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Market research is the systematic process of gathering, analysing, and interpreting information about a market, customers, and competitors to support business decision-making and reduce uncertainty.')
sp()

h2('Primary Research Advantages (10-mark set)')
body('Q: Explain two advantages of primary research. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[An] Specific to the business need: Primary research is designed and conducted to answer the exact questions the business has. [An] Unlike secondary data, which may be general or outdated, primary research provides precisely targeted information that directly informs the specific decision being made.')
bullet('[An] Up-to-date: Primary research captures current customer opinions, attitudes, and behaviour. [An] In fast-changing markets, recent data is essential for accurate decision-making. Secondary data may be months or years old and no longer reflect the current market situation.')
sp()

h2('Secondary Research Reasons (10-mark set)')
body('Q: Explain two reasons a business might use secondary research. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[An] Lower cost and faster: Secondary data already exists (government statistics, industry reports, competitor websites), so collecting it is much cheaper and quicker than conducting primary research. [An] For a small business with a limited budget, secondary research provides useful market intelligence at minimal cost.')
bullet('[An] Provides context and background: Secondary research helps understand the overall market size, growth trends, and competitive landscape before investing in expensive primary research. [An] This background helps design better primary research questions and assess whether a market opportunity exists at all.')
sp()

h2('CRM Benefits (Nov 2024 P12 Q6a)')
body('Q: Explain two benefits of CRM (Customer Relationship Marketing) for a hotel. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] CRM uses data and technology to build long-term relationships with customers, tracking preferences and interactions to personalise service.')
bullet('[App/An] Personalised service: CRM systems record guest preferences (room type, dietary requirements, pillow preferences). [An] When a returning guest is recognised and their preferences automatically met, they feel valued and are more likely to return, building loyalty and lifetime customer value.')
bullet('[App/An] Targeted marketing: CRM data identifies spending patterns and visit frequency, enabling personalised offers (e.g., special rates for guests who haven\'t visited recently, spa packages for guests who previously used the spa). [An] This targeted communication is more effective than generic marketing, generating higher response rates and revenue per marketing dollar spent.')
sp()

h2('Market Research for Hotel Product Development (Nov 2024 P12 Q6b)')
body('Q: Evaluate the importance of market research in developing new products for a hotel. [11]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Market research gathers information about customer needs, preferences, and market trends to inform business decisions.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Identifies unmet customer needs. [App] Research might reveal guests want spa services, pet-friendly rooms, or conference facilities that the hotel does not currently offer. [An] Investing in these additions based on evidence of demand reduces the risk of providing services nobody wants, protecting the investment.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Tests concepts before launch. [An] Showing potential guests mock-ups of new room designs, menu concepts, or pricing structures allows feedback before expensive implementation. This reduces the risk of investing heavily in products that customers reject.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Identifies competitive gaps. [An] Researching what competitors offer and where they fall short reveals opportunities for differentiation. The hotel can develop unique products that fill gaps competitors have left.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: However, research has limitations. [An] Respondents may say they want luxury features but not actually pay premium prices for them. Stated preferences and actual behaviour often differ, making research findings potentially misleading.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: Market research is expensive and time-consuming. [An] For a small independent hotel, the cost of professional research may exceed the budget available. Also, by the time research is completed, market conditions may have changed.')
bullet('[Ev] JUDGEMENT: Market research is important for reducing risk in product development but is not infallible. For a hotel, combining formal research (surveys, focus groups) with informal customer feedback (reviews, complaints, staff observations) provides a comprehensive picture at manageable cost. The importance of formal research increases with the size of the investment - minor additions can rely on informal feedback, while major developments (spa, conference centre) justify formal research expenditure.')
sp()

h2('Niche Cake Business Failure (March 2023 P12 Q6b)')
body('Q: Evaluate why a niche cake business might fail. [11]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] A niche business targets a small, specific market segment rather than the mass market.')
bullet('[An] Limited market size: By definition, a niche market is small. [An] If the local area cannot support enough customers for specialist cakes (e.g., vegan, gluten-free, or luxury celebration cakes), revenue will be insufficient to cover costs regardless of product quality.')
bullet('[An] Vulnerability to competition: If the niche proves profitable, larger competitors or other small businesses may enter. [An] A small cake business has limited resources to defend its position against competitors with bigger marketing budgets or economies of scale in ingredient purchasing.')
bullet('[An] Cash flow problems: Specialist cakes may be ordered intermittently (celebrations, events) rather than providing steady daily income. [An] Periods with few orders still incur rent, wages, and ingredient waste costs, creating cash flow difficulties.')
bullet('[An] HOWEVER - reasons for success: Strong customer loyalty in a niche (reputation for quality, personal service). [An] Premium pricing possible for specialist products. Low competition if the niche is too small to attract large rivals. Word-of-mouth marketing reduces promotional costs.')
bullet('[Ev] JUDGEMENT: A niche cake business is most likely to fail if: the market is too small for the cost base, the owner underestimates financial management challenges, or if larger competitors enter the segment. Success requires a sufficiently large target market, excellent product quality, strong personal brand, careful financial management, and potentially diversification (classes, wholesale, online sales) to supplement core income.')
sp()

