h1('14. MARKETING MIX - PRICE')
body('Penetration, skimming, competitive, price discrimination.')
sp()

h2('Price Discrimination (10-mark set)')
body('Q: Define price discrimination and explain one disadvantage. [3]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Price discrimination is charging different prices to different customers or market segments for the same product, where the price difference is not justified by cost differences (e.g., adult/child tickets, peak/off-peak pricing).')
bullet('[An] Disadvantage: Customers paying the higher price may feel unfairly treated if they discover others pay less for the same product. [An] This can damage brand loyalty and trust, and in some cases may face legal challenges or negative publicity.')
sp()

h2('Competitive Pricing Disadvantage (10-mark set)')
body('Q: Explain one disadvantage of competitive pricing. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Competitive pricing means setting prices at a similar level to competitors.')
bullet('[An] Disadvantage: It may lead to very low profit margins as businesses match each other downwards, particularly if a price war develops. [An] The business loses the ability to charge premium prices and has no pricing advantage, meaning it must compete on other elements of the marketing mix instead.')
sp()

h2('Penetration Pricing for Computer Manufacturer (June 2021 P12 Q5b)')
body('Q: Evaluate whether penetration pricing is the best strategy for a computer manufacturer launching a new product. [11]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Penetration pricing sets a low initial price to gain rapid market share, with the intention of raising prices later once a customer base is established.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Rapidly builds market share. [App] In the competitive computer market, consumers compare prices carefully. A low price attracts price-sensitive buyers who might otherwise choose an established brand. [An] Once they experience the product quality, they become loyal and may accept price increases later.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Deters competitors from entering. [An] The low price makes the market segment appear unprofitable to potential rivals, discouraging entry and protecting the manufacturer emerging market position.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: However, in the technology market, low price often signals low quality. [An] Computer buyers may associate the cheap price with inferior components, unreliable performance, or poor after-sales support. This damages brand perception and may deter the target market.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: Low prices generate thin margins or even losses initially. [An] If the manufacturer cannot absorb these losses while building market share, cash flow problems could threaten survival before the strategy pays off. Computer development costs are high and need recouping.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: Price skimming might be better for innovative products. [An] If the computer has genuinely innovative features, early adopters will pay premium prices. Penetration pricing throws away this premium and the revenue it provides for continued R&D.')
bullet('[Ev] JUDGEMENT: Penetration pricing suits a computer manufacturer entering an established market segment with a "me-too" product, where the main barrier to gaining customers is their existing brand loyalty to competitors. However, for an innovative product with unique features, price skimming is preferable. The choice depends on how differentiated the product is, the target market price sensitivity, and the manufacturer financial capacity to sustain initial low margins.')
sp()

h2('Price Skimming Benefit (Case Study - Custom Motorcycles Nov 2023 P23)')
body('Q: Explain one benefit of price skimming for CM launching a new custom motorcycle model. [3]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Price skimming sets a high initial price for a new/innovative product, targeting customers willing to pay premium prices before gradually reducing price.')
bullet('[App/An] Benefit: Custom motorcycle enthusiasts and early adopters are willing to pay premium prices for exclusive, limited-availability models. [An] Skimming maximises revenue from these customers who value exclusivity and novelty, helping CM recoup the significant R&D and tooling costs of developing a new model quickly.')
sp()

h2('Lowest Prices for New Airline (Nov 2023 P12 Q6b)')
body('Q: Evaluate whether always having the lowest prices is the best strategy for a new airline. [11]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Being the lowest-price option means competing primarily on cost/price rather than service quality or differentiation.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Attracts price-sensitive customers. [An] Many travellers, especially leisure passengers and budget-conscious business travellers, choose purely on price. Being cheapest guarantees demand and rapid passenger growth, building market share quickly.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Fills seats. [An] Airlines have very high fixed costs (aircraft leases, staff, landing fees) regardless of passenger numbers. Selling cheap seats is better than flying with empty seats, as any revenue above marginal cost per passenger contributes to fixed costs.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: However, lowest prices may be unsustainable. [An] A new airline has limited financial reserves. If prices are set below total average cost to attract customers, the airline makes losses. Without sufficient capital reserves, it could fail before reaching profitability.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: Creates a "race to the bottom". [An] Competitors (especially established low-cost carriers with greater financial resources) may match or undercut prices, starting a price war the new entrant cannot win. Established airlines can sustain losses longer.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: Low prices signal low quality. [An] Some passengers avoid the cheapest options fearing poor safety, terrible service, or hidden charges. The brand becomes associated with "cheap" making it difficult to raise prices later or attract premium passengers.')
bullet('[Ev] JUDGEMENT: Having the lowest prices can work as an initial penetration strategy to build passenger numbers quickly, but it is unlikely to be sustainable as the ONLY strategy long-term. The new airline should combine competitive (but not always lowest) pricing with acceptable service quality and route convenience. Once a customer base and reputation are established, prices can be optimised for profitability rather than always being cheapest. Value-for-money rather than cheapest is a more sustainable positioning.')
sp()

h2('Marketing-Corporate Objectives Link (Nov 2023 P12 Q6a)')
body('Q: Explain the link between marketing objectives and corporate objectives. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Corporate objectives are the overall business goals (e.g., 15% profit growth, expand internationally). Marketing objectives are the specific marketing targets that contribute to achieving them.')
bullet('[An] Marketing objectives should be derived from corporate objectives. [An] For example, if the corporate objective is 20% revenue growth, marketing objectives might include: increase market share by 5%, launch 3 new products, enter 2 new geographic markets. Each marketing objective is a step towards delivering the corporate goal.')
bullet('[An] This alignment ensures marketing activity directly supports business strategy rather than pursuing its own agenda. [An] Without this link, marketing might pursue brand awareness goals that do not translate into the revenue growth that shareholders and the board require.')
sp()

h2('Price vs Promotion in Marketing Mix (Case Study - Great Resources)')
body('Q: Evaluate whether price or promotion is more important in the marketing mix for GR. [11]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Price is the amount charged to customers. Promotion is communication activities to inform and persuade customers.')
bullet('[An] Price importance: GR operates in a competitive market where customers compare prices. [An] Setting the wrong price (too high - loses customers to rivals; too low - insufficient margin) directly affects both revenue and profit. Price must reflect value, cover costs, and be competitive.')
bullet('[An] Promotion importance: Even a well-priced product will not sell if customers do not know about it. [App] For GR, promoting to the right target market builds awareness and generates enquiries. [An] Effective promotion creates brand recognition, communicates the value proposition, and drives customer traffic.')
bullet('[An] However, promotion costs money and the return is uncertain. [An] Spending heavily on promotion for a poorly-priced product wastes resources - customers attracted by promotion will not buy if the price is wrong.')
bullet('[Ev] JUDGEMENT: Both are important and interdependent. Price must be right to convert interest into sales; promotion must be effective to generate interest in the first place. For GR, the relative importance depends on its market position: if products are well-known but losing sales to cheaper rivals, price is more important. If products are good value but unknown, promotion is more important. In practice, both must be appropriate simultaneously for marketing mix success.')
sp()

