h2('Digital Promotion Benefits (Case Study - Jake\'s Cakes Nov 2023 P23)')
body('Q: Explain two benefits of digital promotion for JC. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[App/An] Visual showcase: Cake businesses benefit hugely from visual platforms (Instagram, Pinterest, TikTok). [An] High-quality photos and videos of beautifully decorated cakes attract customers and demonstrate skill/quality in a way that text advertising cannot match. Potential customers can instantly see JC work and decide to order.')
bullet('[App/An] Low cost and targeted reach: Digital advertising can be targeted to local users searching for "birthday cakes" or "wedding cakes" in the area. [An] This is much cheaper than newspaper advertising and reaches exactly the people most likely to buy, maximising return on promotional spending for a small business with limited budget.')
sp()

h2('Sampling Limitations (March 2024 P12 Q5a)')
body('Q: Explain two limitations of sampling as a promotion method for a parcel delivery service. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Sampling means giving potential customers a free trial of the product/service to encourage future purchase.')
bullet('[App/An] High cost per sample: Delivering parcels free as samples means bearing the full cost of delivery (driver, fuel, vehicle, insurance) without revenue. [An] For a delivery service where each transaction has significant variable costs, free trials are expensive. Unlike sampling a food product (pennies per sample), each delivery trial could cost several dollars.')
bullet('[App/An] Customers may only use the free service without converting to paying customers. [An] Some people will take the free delivery but revert to their existing provider afterwards, particularly if the existing provider is adequate. The conversion rate from free trial to loyal customer may be low, making the cost per acquired customer very high.')
sp()

h2('Promotion for Parcel Delivery (March 2024 P12 Q5b)')
body('Q: Evaluate the most effective promotion method for a parcel delivery service. [11]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Promotion methods include advertising, sales promotion, direct marketing, digital marketing, and public relations.')
bullet('[An] Digital advertising (Google Ads, social media): Targets people actively searching for delivery services or running online businesses that need deliveries. [An] Pay-per-click means the business only pays when someone shows interest. Can be targeted geographically and by customer type (businesses vs consumers), making it cost-effective and measurable.')
bullet('[An] B2B direct marketing: Approaching online retailers and businesses directly with offers of competitive delivery rates. [An] Since business customers generate regular, repeat orders (unlike one-off consumers), converting one business customer provides ongoing revenue. Personal relationship building through sales representatives is effective in B2B markets.')
bullet('[An] Customer referral programmes: Offering existing satisfied customers a discount for referring new customers. [An] This leverages trust (people believe recommendations from people they know) and only costs money when new customers are actually acquired. However, growth is slow and depends on having satisfied existing customers first.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST mass media advertising: TV/radio advertising reaches many people but most do not need delivery services. [An] The cost per relevant customer reached is high, making mass media inefficient for a service with a specific target market.')
bullet('[Ev] JUDGEMENT: For a parcel delivery service, digital advertising combined with B2B direct marketing is likely most effective. Digital reaches consumers and small businesses searching for solutions online; direct marketing secures larger business accounts. The balance depends on whether the target is consumers (digital focus) or businesses (direct/relationship focus). Mass media is likely too expensive and poorly targeted for this service.')
sp()

h2('Branding for Soft Drink (Nov 2022 P13 Q5b)')
body('Q: Evaluate the importance of branding for a soft drink company. [11]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Branding is the process of creating a distinctive name, image, and identity for a product to differentiate it from competitors.')
bullet('[An] FOR: In the soft drink market, products are physically very similar (flavoured, carbonated water with sugar). [An] Branding is what creates perceived differences - Coca-Cola and a supermarket cola are chemically similar, but branding allows Coca-Cola to charge a premium. Without branding, soft drinks become commodities competing solely on price.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Builds emotional connection and loyalty. [An] Strong soft drink brands create loyalty that survives price increases, promotional activity by competitors, and even product changes. Customers feel connected to the brand identity, not just the liquid.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Supports new product launches. [An] A trusted brand can extend into new flavours, diet versions, or energy drinks with lower marketing costs because existing brand equity provides instant credibility and customer interest.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: However, branding requires enormous ongoing investment. [An] Maintaining a soft drink brand requires constant advertising, sponsorship, social media presence, and packaging refreshes. Small or new companies cannot match established brand spend, limiting their ability to compete.')
bullet('[Ev] JUDGEMENT: Branding is critically important in the soft drinks market because the physical product is easily replicated - branding IS the competitive advantage for market leaders. For small or new entrants, building a brand is challenging and expensive, so they may compete initially on niche positioning (organic, health-focused, local) rather than trying to out-brand established global players. However, even niche players need some form of brand identity to differentiate and justify premium pricing.')
sp()

h2('Marketing for Motor Car Manufacturer (Nov 2022 P12 Q5b)')
body('Q: Evaluate the marketing strategy a motor car manufacturer should use. [11]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Marketing strategy encompasses targeting, positioning, and the marketing mix decisions that deliver competitive advantage.')
bullet('[An] Product: Cars must be reliable, safe, well-designed, and meet emission standards. [An] Product quality and features are essential because cars are expensive purchases that customers research extensively. Poor reviews or reliability issues destroy sales quickly in the digital age.')
bullet('[An] Price: Must match the brand positioning (luxury vs economy) and compete within the segment. [An] A mid-range manufacturer must price competitively against similar brands while maintaining margins for dealer incentives and after-sales support.')
bullet('[An] Promotion: Major investment required in advertising (TV, digital, showroom experience). [An] Car purchases involve long decision-making processes - promotion must work at awareness stage (advertising) and conversion stage (test drives, finance offers, showroom experience).')
bullet('[An] Place: Dealer network is crucial - customers want to see, touch, and test drive before buying. [An] However, digital sales channels are growing. The balance between traditional dealerships and online/direct sales is a key strategic decision.')
bullet('[Ev] JUDGEMENT: For a motor car manufacturer, no single element dominates - the marketing mix must be coherent. However, Product is arguably foundational because in a market with extensive reviews and consumer information, a poor product cannot be rescued by good promotion or pricing. The most effective strategy aligns product quality with appropriate pricing, supported by impactful promotion through a convenient distribution network. The specific emphasis depends on brand positioning (luxury emphasises product/promotion; budget emphasises price/place).')
sp()

