h1('18. OPERATIONS - INVENTORY MANAGEMENT')
body('JIT, JIC, inventory control, lead time.')
sp()

h2('JIT Benefits (Case Study - Motorcycle Components June 2022 P21)')
body('Q: Explain two benefits of JIT (Just-in-Time) inventory management for MC. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] JIT means receiving materials only when needed for production, holding minimal or zero inventory.')
bullet('[App/An] Reduced storage costs: MC does not need large warehouses to store motorcycle components. [An] This saves rent, insurance, security, and handling costs. For precision engineering components that may be expensive, reducing inventory also frees up significant working capital.')
bullet('[App/An] Reduced waste from obsolescence: Motorcycle components may be updated or redesigned frequently. [An] With JIT, MC does not risk holding large stocks of components that become obsolete when designs change, avoiding the write-off costs that come with outdated inventory.')
sp()

h2('JIT (Case Study - Great Desks June 2023 P23)')
body('Q: Explain what is meant by JIT and give one advantage for GD. [3]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] JIT (Just-in-Time) is an inventory management system where materials are delivered just before they are needed in production, minimising stock held.')
bullet('[App/An] For GD (custom furniture maker): Wood and materials arrive shortly before each bespoke piece is made. [An] This reduces cash tied up in stock (which is important for a small business), minimises storage space needed, and ensures materials are fresh and undamaged from long storage. Since each piece is custom, holding standard stock would be wasteful as requirements vary per order.')
sp()

h2('JIT Impacts (Case Study - Bear Bears Nov 2023 P22)')
body('Q: Analyse the impact of adopting JIT on BB (teddy bear manufacturer). [6]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[App/An] Positive - Reduced storage costs: BB no longer needs warehouse space for large stocks of fabric, stuffing, and accessories. [An] This frees up space (potentially reducing rent) and capital for other uses such as marketing or product development.')
bullet('[App/An] Positive - Better cash flow: Money is not tied up in unused materials sitting in storage. [An] Cash can be used for other purposes or reduces the need for overdraft facilities, saving interest costs.')
bullet('[App/An] Negative - Vulnerability to supply disruption: If fabric or component suppliers are late delivering, BB production line stops. [An] Unlike holding buffer stock, JIT offers no protection against late deliveries, transport problems, or supplier failures. Any production stoppage means customer orders are delayed, potentially losing sales.')
bullet('[App/An] Negative - May lose bulk-buying discounts: Ordering smaller, more frequent deliveries may cost more per unit than large bulk orders. [An] Suppliers may charge higher unit prices for small quantities or higher delivery charges for frequent shipments, potentially increasing variable costs.')
sp()

h2('Holding High Inventory Benefits (June 2023 P11 Q5a)')
body('Q: Explain two benefits of holding high levels of inventory. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[An] Ability to meet unexpected demand: If customer orders suddenly increase, high inventory means the business can fulfil them immediately without delay. [An] This prevents lost sales, maintains customer satisfaction, and avoids the reputational damage of being unable to supply. Competitors with stock available will capture orders from businesses that cannot fulfil them.')
bullet('[An] Protection against supply disruption: High inventory provides a buffer if suppliers face problems (strikes, transport delays, quality issues, natural disasters). [An] Production can continue from stock while alternative supply is arranged, preventing costly production line shutdowns.')
bullet('[An] Bulk buying discounts: Purchasing large quantities to hold in stock usually attracts lower unit prices from suppliers. [An] The savings on purchase price may exceed the additional holding costs (storage, insurance, handling), improving overall margins.')
sp()

h2('JIC Purpose (Short Answers)')
body('Q: Explain the purpose of JIC (Just-in-Case) inventory management. [3]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] JIC means holding high levels of buffer stock to ensure materials are always available when needed, regardless of demand fluctuations or supply uncertainties.')
bullet('[An] Purpose: To guarantee continuous production by protecting against supply disruption, demand surges, and lead time uncertainties. This is appropriate when supply is unreliable, demand is unpredictable, or stockout costs are very high (e.g., hospital supplies, seasonal production).')
sp()

h2('Lead Time (Case Study - Motorcycle Components June 2022 P21)')
body('Q: Define lead time. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Lead time is the period between placing an order with a supplier and receiving the goods. It includes the time for order processing, production/picking, and delivery. Shorter lead times allow lower inventory levels; longer lead times require earlier ordering and potentially higher buffer stocks.')
sp()

h2('Inventory (Case Study - Cartoon Costumes Nov 2022 P22)')
body('Q: Explain what is meant by inventory for a business like CC. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Inventory refers to all the stock held by a business at any point in time. For CC this includes: raw materials (fabric, thread, accessories), work-in-progress (partially completed costumes), and finished goods (completed costumes ready for sale).')
sp()

h2('Mission Statement (Case Study - Motorcycle Components June 2022 P21)')
body('Q: Explain one benefit of MC having a mission statement. [3]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] A mission statement is a brief description of the business overall purpose and values.')
bullet('[An] Benefit: It provides direction and focus for all employees, ensuring everyone understands and works towards the same overall purpose. [An] For MC, a mission statement emphasising "precision engineering and quality" guides decision-making at all levels - from procurement (choosing quality materials over cheapest) to production (not cutting corners) to sales (targeting quality-focused customers).')
sp()

# ===========================================================================
# TOPIC 19: OPERATIONS - PRODUCTION METHODS
# ===========================================================================
h1('19. OPERATIONS - PRODUCTION METHODS')
body('Job, batch, flow, mass customisation.')
sp()

h2('Job Production (Case Study - Great Desks June 2023 P23)')
body('Q: Explain what is meant by job production. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Job production is when each product is made individually to a specific customer order/specification. Each item is unique (or significantly different from others), made one at a time from start to finish. Examples include custom furniture, wedding cakes, and bespoke tailoring.')
sp()

h2('Batch Production (Case Study - Planting Pots Nov 2022 P23)')
body('Q: Define batch production. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Batch production is when a group (batch) of identical products is made together, moving through each stage of production as a unit. The equipment is then reset to produce the next batch of a different product. Examples include bakeries (batch of bread, then batch of cakes) and clothing (batch of size 10, then size 12).')
sp()

h2('Mass Customisation (Case Study - Bear Bears Nov 2023 P22)')
body('Q: Define mass customisation. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Mass customisation combines the efficiency of mass/flow production with the personalisation of job production. Products are manufactured using standardised processes but customers can choose specific features/options (colours, accessories, engravings) to create a semi-unique product at near mass-production cost and speed.')
sp()

h2('Changing Production Method (Case Study - Custom Motorcycles Nov 2023 P23)')
body('Q: Evaluate the impact on CM of changing from job production to batch production. [11]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] CM currently makes each motorcycle individually (job production) and is considering producing batches of similar models.')
bullet('[An] FOR - Lower costs: Batch production allows some economies of scale. [An] Buying components in larger quantities for a batch of 20 identical bikes costs less per unit than buying for one bike. Workers specialise in specific tasks, becoming faster. Setup time is shared across the batch rather than incurred for each individual bike.')
bullet('[An] FOR - Faster output: Producing batches means more motorcycles completed per month. [An] This allows CM to grow, serve more customers, and increase revenue without proportionally increasing costs. Waiting times for customers reduce, improving satisfaction.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST - Loss of USP: CM differentiation is based on unique, custom motorcycles. [An] Moving to batch production means customers receive one of several standard models rather than a one-off creation. The premium pricing and brand prestige that comes from uniqueness may be lost, and customers may switch to other custom builders.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST - Worker demotivation: Skilled craftspeople who take pride in building complete motorcycles may be demotivated by repetitive batch tasks. [An] Job enrichment and satisfaction from seeing a unique creation come together is lost. This may cause skilled workers to leave, reducing quality and requiring costly recruitment.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST - Capital investment needed: Switching to batch production requires investment in different equipment, workspace layout changes, and potentially new technology. [An] This is expensive and risky if the market does not respond positively to batch-produced bikes.')
bullet('[Ev] JUDGEMENT: The impact depends on CM target market and brand positioning. If the market demands more accessible, affordable motorcycles, batch production enables growth and lower prices. However, if CM brand value is built on exclusivity and craftsmanship, switching to batch production could destroy its competitive advantage. A compromise might be to produce standard models in batches while maintaining a premium custom service for those willing to pay extra - a two-tier approach that captures both market segments.')
sp()

h2('Batch to Mass Customisation Impact (Case Study - Fretter\'s Music June 2023 P23)')
body('Q: Analyse the impact on stakeholders of FM changing from batch production to mass customisation. [6]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[App/An] Customers: Benefit from being able to personalise instruments (colour, specification, engraving) while still receiving them relatively quickly at affordable prices. [An] Previously, customers chose from standard batch-produced models or paid premium for full custom. Mass customisation gives personalisation at near-batch prices, improving customer satisfaction and loyalty.')
bullet('[App/An] Employees: May need retraining to operate new flexible manufacturing systems. [An] Some may find the change stimulating (variety of specifications to produce), while others may lose satisfaction if the process becomes more automated. Some workers may face redundancy if technology replaces their roles.')
bullet('[App/An] Shareholders: Investment in flexible manufacturing technology is initially expensive, reducing short-term profits/dividends. [An] However, if mass customisation attracts more customers and allows premium pricing for personalisation, long-term profitability should increase, benefiting shareholders through higher future returns.')
sp()

h2('New Machinery Limitation (Case Study - Benjamin\'s Beds March 2022 P21)')
body('Q: Explain one limitation of buying new machinery for BB. [3]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[An] High capital cost: New bed-making machinery requires significant upfront investment. [An] This reduces available cash flow (or requires borrowing with interest costs), and the machinery may take years to generate sufficient productivity savings to pay for itself. If demand falls before payback, the investment represents a loss.')
bullet('[An] Staff retraining needed: Workers must learn to operate new machinery, taking time away from production. [An] During the transition period, productivity may actually fall while workers learn, and some experienced workers may resist change or leave if they feel their skills are no longer valued.')
sp()

h2('B2B vs B2C Recommendation (Case Study - Benjamin\'s Beds March 2022 P21)')
body('Q: Recommend whether BB should focus on B2B or B2C sales. [6]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] B2B (Business-to-Business) means selling to other businesses (hotels, retailers). B2C (Business-to-Consumer) means selling directly to individual customers.')
bullet('[App/An] B2B advantages: Large, regular orders from hotels or furniture retailers provide predictable revenue and allow efficient batch production. [An] One large contract can fill production capacity, reducing sales and marketing costs per unit. However, dependence on few large customers creates vulnerability if they switch supplier.')
bullet('[App/An] B2C advantages: Direct-to-consumer sales (online, own showroom) allow higher margins by eliminating retailer markup. [An] BB can build brand awareness and customer relationships directly. However, B2C requires investment in marketing, delivery logistics, and customer service that B2B does not.')
bullet('[Ev] Recommendation: BB should pursue both channels but with different product strategies. B2B for standard bed models (efficient batch production, lower margins but high volume). B2C for premium/custom beds (higher margins, brand building). This diversifies revenue streams and reduces dependence on either channel. The balance depends on BB production capacity and marketing resources.')
sp()

