h1('20. OPERATIONS - CAPACITY & OUTSOURCING')
body('Capacity utilisation, outsourcing.')
sp()

h2('Below Maximum Capacity Impact (Short Answers)')
body('Q: Explain the impact on a business of operating below maximum capacity. [3]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[An] Higher average fixed costs per unit: Fixed costs (rent, machinery depreciation, management salaries) are spread over fewer units than the factory was designed for. [An] This means each unit bears a higher share of fixed costs, increasing average total cost per unit and reducing profit margins or competitiveness.')
bullet('[An] Wasted resources: Equipment, space, and potentially staff are underutilised, representing wasted investment. [An] The business pays for capacity it does not use, reducing return on capital employed.')
sp()

h2('Over Maximum Capacity Impact (Short Answers)')
body('Q: Explain the impact of operating over maximum capacity. [3]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[An] Increased strain on machinery: Running equipment beyond designed capacity leads to more frequent breakdowns and accelerated wear. [An] This increases maintenance costs, causes production stoppages, and may result in expensive emergency repairs or premature replacement needs.')
bullet('[An] Worker stress and quality issues: Staff working overtime, extra shifts, or at rushed pace become tired and demotivated. [An] This leads to higher error rates, more defective output, increased absenteeism, and higher labour turnover. Quality suffers as workers cut corners to meet unrealistic targets.')
sp()

h2('Improving Capacity Utilisation Benefit (Short Answers)')
body('Q: Explain one benefit of improving capacity utilisation. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[An] Lower average cost per unit: As more units are produced using the same fixed capacity (premises, equipment), fixed costs are spread over more units. [An] This reduces average total cost, either improving profit margins at the same price or allowing price reductions to increase competitiveness and market share.')
sp()

h2('Outsourcing Definition (Short Answers)')
body('Q: Define outsourcing and give an example. [3]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Outsourcing is when a business contracts out a function or process to an external specialist company rather than performing it in-house. [K] Examples include: outsourcing IT support to a technology firm, outsourcing cleaning to a facilities company, outsourcing payroll to an accountancy firm, or outsourcing manufacturing to a contract producer.')
sp()

h2('Outsourcing (Short Answers)')
body('Q: Explain two advantages and two disadvantages of outsourcing. [6]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[An] Advantage 1 - Access to specialist expertise: The outsourcing partner specialises in that function, so likely performs it better and more efficiently than the business could internally. [An] For example, an IT specialist firm has deeper expertise, better equipment, and more current knowledge than a small in-house team could achieve.')
bullet('[An] Advantage 2 - Cost reduction: Converting fixed costs (permanent staff, equipment) into variable costs (pay only for what you use). [An] The business avoids recruitment, training, and overhead costs for functions that are not core to its competitive advantage.')
bullet('[An] Disadvantage 1 - Loss of control: The business depends on an external party for quality, timing, and reliability. [An] If the outsourcing partner underperforms, it directly affects the business customers, but the business has limited ability to intervene compared to managing its own staff.')
bullet('[An] Disadvantage 2 - Security/confidentiality risks: Sharing business information, customer data, or processes with external companies creates risks. [An] Sensitive information may be exposed, and the outsourcing partner may work with competitors, potentially leaking proprietary knowledge.')
sp()

h2('Opening New Factory Evaluation (Case Study - Delicious Cocoa June 2023 P21)')
body('Q: Evaluate whether DC should open a new factory. [11]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Opening a new factory represents major capital investment and a long-term strategic commitment to growth.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Increased capacity to meet growing demand. [App] If DC is operating at or near full capacity, it cannot grow without additional production space. [An] A new factory removes this constraint, allowing DC to accept larger orders, enter new markets, and increase revenue significantly.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Economies of scale. [An] A larger operation can negotiate better prices for cocoa beans and other inputs, spread marketing and management costs over more output, and invest in more efficient technology. Lower unit costs improve competitiveness and margins.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Geographic diversification. [An] A factory in a different location (perhaps closer to customers or raw material sources) reduces transport costs and supply chain risk. If one factory has problems, the other can maintain some production.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: Massive capital investment with uncertain return. [An] Building and equipping a factory costs millions, funded by loans (interest payments) or share issues (ownership dilution). If demand does not grow as expected, DC has expensive unused capacity and debt obligations.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: Management complexity. [An] Running multiple sites is more complex than one - coordination, communication, quality consistency, and recruitment across locations all become more difficult. Diseconomies of scale may emerge.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: Risk in current economic climate. [An] Economic uncertainty, changing consumer preferences, and potential supply chain disruption make large investments risky. A more cautious approach (e.g., outsourcing production, extending current factory hours) might achieve growth with less risk.')
bullet('[Ev] JUDGEMENT: DC should open a new factory only if: demand forecasts are strong and well-researched, finance is available at acceptable cost, and management has capacity to run multiple sites. The investment should be phased (start smaller, expand if demand proves real) rather than committing to maximum capacity immediately. Alternative growth strategies (outsourcing, partnerships, shift patterns) should be compared on a cost-benefit basis before committing to the highest-risk option.')
sp()

h2('Opening New Location Evaluation (Case Study - Move Well Nov 2022 P21)')
body('Q: Evaluate whether MW should open a new fitness studio location. [11]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Opening a new location means replicating the business model in a different geographic area.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Growth in revenue and market reach. [An] A second location serves customers in a different area who cannot travel to the original. This doubles potential market reach and revenue without cannibalising existing customers.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Economies of scale in marketing and brand. [An] Marketing costs (website, social media, brand development) can be shared across both locations. The brand becomes more recognisable with multiple locations, building credibility and trust.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: High fixed costs and risk. [An] Rent, equipment, utilities, and staff for a second location represent significant ongoing costs regardless of customer numbers. If the new location fails to attract sufficient customers, losses can threaten the entire business.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: Management stretch. [An] The owner cannot be in two places at once. Quality and culture may suffer at the location without direct owner supervision. Hiring a manager adds cost and introduces delegation risks.')
bullet('[Ev] JUDGEMENT: MW should open a new location only after the first is consistently profitable and the owner has developed systems (class schedules, quality standards, staff management) that can be replicated without their constant presence. Market research should confirm demand in the proposed location. Starting with a smaller/cheaper space (testing demand) before committing to a large lease reduces risk. Alternatives (more classes at existing location, online classes) should also be considered as lower-risk growth options.')
sp()

