h2('Working Capital Calculation (Case Study - Cartoon Costumes Nov 2022 P22)')
body('Q: CC has current assets of $15,000 and current liabilities of $9,000. Calculate working capital. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[Calc] Working capital = Current assets - Current liabilities = $15,000 - $9,000 = $6,000')
bullet('[An] CC has $6,000 available for day-to-day operations after meeting short-term obligations. This appears adequate but depends on the scale of operations and timing of cash flows.')
sp()

h2('Market Share Calculation (Case Study - Benjamin\'s Beds March 2022 P21)')
body('Q: Define market share. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Market share is the proportion (percentage) of total market sales that is captured by one business. Formula: (Business sales / Total market sales) x 100. It indicates the business competitive position relative to rivals in the same market.')
sp()

h2('Opportunity Cost (Case Study - Office Furniture Designs June 2023 P22)')
body('Q: Define opportunity cost. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative that is given up when a decision is made. For example, if OFD spends $50,000 on new machinery, the opportunity cost might be the marketing campaign or staff training that the money could otherwise have funded.')
sp()

h2('Corporate Objectives (Case Study - Move Well Nov 2022 P21)')
body('Q: Define corporate objectives. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Corporate objectives are the specific, measurable goals set for the whole organisation, derived from its mission statement and aims. They provide direction for all departments and are used to measure overall business performance. Examples include profit targets, market share goals, and growth objectives.')
sp()

h2('Internal Growth (Case Study - Move Well Nov 2022 P21)')
body('Q: Define internal (organic) growth. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Internal (organic) growth is expansion achieved using the business own resources and capabilities, such as opening new locations, developing new products, hiring more staff, or investing in increased capacity. It does not involve merging with or acquiring other businesses.')
sp()

h2('Redundant Definition (Case Study - Seaside Hotel June 2022 P22)')
body('Q: Define redundancy. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Redundancy occurs when an employee job ceases to exist because it is no longer needed by the business. This may be due to closure, restructuring, automation, or declining demand. The employee is dismissed not because of their performance but because the role itself is eliminated.')
sp()

h2('Training Type (Case Study - Gold Theme Park Nov 2023 P22)')
body('Q: Identify and explain one appropriate type of training for new GT staff. [3]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[App/An] Induction training: New theme park staff need immediate training on health and safety procedures, emergency protocols, ride operation procedures, and customer service standards. [An] This is essential before they begin work because theme parks involve significant safety risks (rides, crowds, food handling). Untrained staff operating rides or managing crowd safety could cause serious accidents and legal liability.')
sp()

h2('Direct Costs (Case Study - Gold Theme Park Nov 2023 P22)')
body('Q: Define direct costs and give an example for GT. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Direct costs are costs that can be directly traced to and identified with a specific product, service, or activity. [App] For GT, direct costs include: ride maintenance costs (directly linked to specific rides), food ingredients (directly linked to food sales), and performer wages (directly linked to entertainment shows).')
sp()

h2('Secondary Research Sources (Case Study - Gold Theme Park Nov 2023 P22)')
body('Q: Explain two sources of secondary research GT could use. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[App/An] Tourism industry reports: Published data on visitor numbers, spending patterns, and tourism trends from government tourism bodies or industry associations. [An] This helps GT understand market growth, seasonal patterns, and visitor demographics without the cost of conducting its own research.')
bullet('[App/An] Competitor websites and social media: Reviewing other theme parks pricing, new attractions, and customer reviews provides competitive intelligence. [An] This is free and readily available, helping GT benchmark its offering and identify gaps or opportunities that competitors are exploiting.')
sp()

h2('Intrapreneurship Importance (Case Study - Gold Theme Park Nov 2023 P22)')
body('Q: Explain the importance of intrapreneurship for GT. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Intrapreneurship means employees within GT generating innovative ideas for new attractions, services, or operational improvements.')
bullet('[App/An] Theme parks must constantly innovate to attract repeat visitors. [An] If employees are encouraged to develop new ride concepts, event ideas, or operational improvements, GT stays fresh and exciting without relying solely on expensive external consultants or management ideas.')
bullet('[An] Employees working daily with visitors often identify opportunities that management misses (e.g., gaps in food offerings, queue management improvements, new merchandise ideas). [An] Harnessing this frontline knowledge through intrapreneurship creates continuous improvement at low cost.')
sp()

h2('Decreased Staff Morale Impacts (Case Study - Benjamin\'s Beds March 2022 P21)')
body('Q: Explain two impacts on BB of decreased staff morale. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[An] Lower productivity: Demotivated workers put less effort into their work, working more slowly and taking less care. [App] For BB, this means fewer beds produced per day and potentially lower quality craftsmanship. [An] Lower productivity increases cost per bed and may delay customer orders.')
bullet('[An] Higher absenteeism and turnover: Unhappy workers take more sick days and eventually leave for other jobs. [An] This disrupts production schedules, increases recruitment and training costs, and loses experienced workers whose skills and knowledge are difficult to replace quickly.')
sp()

h2('Equality in Workplace Evaluation (Case Study - Bear Bears Nov 2023 P22)')
body('Q: Evaluate the importance of equality in the workplace for BB. [6]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Workplace equality means treating all employees fairly regardless of gender, age, ethnicity, disability, or other protected characteristics.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Legal compliance. [An] Discrimination is illegal in most countries. BB must ensure equal treatment to avoid employment tribunals, fines, and reputational damage from discrimination cases.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Access to best talent. [An] If BB recruits and promotes based on merit rather than bias, it gets the best people for each role. Discriminating against any group artificially limits the talent pool, potentially missing excellent candidates.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Improved morale and productivity. [An] Employees who feel fairly treated are more motivated and committed. Those experiencing discrimination become demotivated, produce poor-quality work, and leave quickly - all costly for the business.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: However, implementing equality measures (training, policy development, monitoring, reporting) takes time and money that a small business like BB may struggle to resource. [An] The owner may feel that as a small team, they already treat everyone fairly without formal policies. But informal approaches risk unconscious bias.')
bullet('[Ev] Both morally and commercially, equality is important for BB. Fair treatment attracts and retains the best workers, avoids legal risks, and creates a positive culture. While formal equality policies have implementation costs, the risks of NOT having them (legal action, poor reputation, limited talent pool) far outweigh the investment.')
sp()

h2('Ethics in Clothing Retail Marketing (Nov 2023 P13 Q5b)')
body('Q: Evaluate the importance of ethical considerations in marketing for a clothing retailer. [11]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Ethical marketing means being honest, transparent, and responsible in all marketing activities - avoiding deception, exploitation, or harm.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Honest advertising builds trust. [An] If a clothing retailer claims products are "sustainable" or "ethically sourced" and this is true, it builds a loyal customer base who trust the brand. Greenwashing (false claims) is increasingly detected by social media and investigative journalism, causing severe reputational damage.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Responsible body image representation. [An] Using diverse models and avoiding unrealistic body standards is both ethical and commercially sensible - customers respond positively to brands that make them feel included rather than inadequate. This builds emotional connection and loyalty.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Fair pricing/no deception. [An] Honest pricing (no misleading "was" prices, genuine sales) builds long-term customer trust. Retailers caught using deceptive pricing face regulatory action, media criticism, and customer boycotts.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: However, strict ethical standards may limit marketing options. [An] Not using "aspirational" imagery, not exaggerating product benefits, and not using high-pressure sales tactics may reduce short-term sales compared to less ethical competitors who use these techniques effectively.')
bullet('[Ev] JUDGEMENT: Ethical marketing is increasingly important for clothing retailers because: consumers are more informed and ethically aware (especially younger demographics), social media exposes unethical practices quickly, and regulatory standards are tightening. While ethical marketing may sacrifice some short-term sales, it builds the long-term brand value, customer loyalty, and reputation that sustain the business. The fashion industry faces particular scrutiny on ethics (fast fashion, body image, sustainability), making ethical marketing a competitive necessity rather than just a moral choice.')
sp()

h2('Motivating Young People (Case Study - Delicious Cocoa June 2023 P21)')
body('Q: Evaluate how DC could motivate young workers in a cocoa factory. [11]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] Young workers (often in their first jobs) may have different motivational needs than experienced workers.')
bullet('[An] Financial motivators: Fair wages are essential as a hygiene factor (Herzberg). [App] Young workers often have immediate financial needs (rent, transport, social life). [An] Competitive wages, performance bonuses, or piece rates directly address these needs and provide clear incentive for effort.')
bullet('[An] Training and development: Young workers value learning new skills and career progression. [An] Offering structured training programmes, apprenticeships, and clear promotion pathways shows DC invests in their future. This satisfies esteem and self-actualisation needs (Maslow) and acts as a motivator (Herzberg - personal growth).')
bullet('[An] Social/team environment: Young workers often highly value social connection at work. [An] Team-based production, social events, and a friendly atmosphere satisfy belonging needs (Maslow) and make work enjoyable. A positive social environment reduces turnover among young workers who might otherwise move on quickly.')
bullet('[An] Recognition and feedback: Regular positive feedback and recognition of achievements. [An] Young workers new to employment particularly value knowing they are performing well. "Employee of the month" schemes, public praise, and constructive feedback build confidence and motivation.')
bullet('[Ev] JUDGEMENT: The most effective approach combines fair pay (addressing basic needs) with development opportunities and a positive social environment. According to Herzberg, pay alone prevents dissatisfaction but does not create true motivation - that comes from achievement, recognition, and growth. For young workers specifically, the social environment and development opportunities are often more important than for older workers who may prioritise job security or work-life balance. DC should offer a package that addresses multiple needs simultaneously.')
sp()

h2('Objectives Changing (Case Study - MX Bikes June 2023 P22)')
body('Q: Explain two reasons why a business might change its objectives. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[An] Change in market conditions: If the market enters recession, growth objectives may be replaced by survival objectives. [App] If the motorcycle market declines due to fuel prices or regulation, MXB might shift from "increase market share" to "maintain profitability." [An] Objectives must be realistic and relevant to current conditions.')
bullet('[An] Change in business stage/size: As a business grows from start-up to established, objectives naturally evolve. [An] A start-up might focus on survival and building reputation; once established, objectives shift to growth, profit maximisation, or market dominance. What was appropriate for a small firm becomes irrelevant for a larger one.')
sp()

h2('Finance Choice Factors (Case Study - Office Furniture Designs June 2023 P22)')
body('Q: Explain one factor that affects the choice of source of finance for OFD. [3]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[An] Purpose and duration of need: If OFD needs finance for a short-term purpose (buying timber for current orders), a short-term source (overdraft, trade credit) is appropriate. If needed for a long-term investment (new workshop, machinery), a long-term source (bank loan, mortgage) matches the repayment to the asset lifespan. [An] Using short-term finance for long-term assets creates repayment pressure before the investment generates returns.')
sp()

h2('Business Plan Element (Case Study - Jake\'s Cakes Nov 2023 P23)')
body('Q: Identify one element of a business plan relevant to JC. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[App] Financial forecast/cash flow forecast: JC business plan would include predictions of monthly income (from cake sales), expenses (ingredients, rent, wages, utilities), and the resulting cash position. [An] This is essential for securing any loan or investment and for ensuring JC has enough funding to survive the initial months before building a customer base.')
sp()

h2('Consumer Market (Case Study - Fretter\'s Music June 2023 P23)')
body('Q: Define consumer market. [2]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] A consumer market (B2C) is one where products and services are sold to individual end-users for personal use rather than to other businesses. FM sells guitars, drums, and music accessories to individual musicians and hobbyists for their personal use.')
sp()

h2('International Market Cost Impacts (Case Study - Fretter\'s Music June 2023 P23)')
body('Q: Explain two cost implications of selling in international markets for FM. [4]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[App/An] Shipping and logistics costs: Sending musical instruments internationally requires specialist packaging (instruments are fragile and valuable), international freight services, and customs documentation. [An] These costs are significantly higher than domestic delivery and may make prices uncompetitive in distant markets unless absorbed or passed to customers.')
bullet('[App/An] Marketing and localisation costs: Reaching customers in different countries requires translated websites, market research into local preferences, and potentially different marketing strategies. [An] These additional costs must be incurred before any international sales are generated, representing a financial risk if international expansion fails to attract sufficient customers.')
sp()

h2('Marketing Mix Element (Case Study - Fretter\'s Music June 2023 P23)')
body('Q: Identify the most important element of the marketing mix for FM. [3]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[App/An] Product is arguably most important for a specialist music retailer: Musicians are knowledgeable and demanding customers who prioritise product quality, brand reputation, and range. [An] If FM does not stock respected instrument brands in good condition and variety, no amount of pricing or promotion will attract serious musicians. The product offering IS the business - customers visit FM specifically for its instrument selection and expertise.')
sp()

h2('Bank Loan Evaluation (Case Study - Jake\'s Cakes Nov 2023 P23)')
body('Q: Evaluate whether a bank loan is the best source of finance for JC to expand. [11]')
sp()
body('A:')
bullet('[K] A bank loan provides a lump sum repaid with interest over a fixed period in regular instalments.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Provides a large sum for significant investment (new oven, larger premises, delivery van). [An] The fixed repayment schedule is predictable, aiding cash flow planning. JC knows exactly what to pay each month.')
bullet('[An] FOR: Retains full ownership - unlike selling shares, a loan does not dilute ownership or give the bank any control over business decisions. [An] JC owner maintains complete autonomy while having access to funds for growth.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: However, interest charges increase total repayment above the amount borrowed. [An] For a small cake business with potentially variable income (seasonal demand, event-dependent orders), fixed loan repayments create pressure even in quiet months when cash flow is tight.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: Banks require security (collateral) for loans. [An] JC may need to pledge personal assets (house, car), creating personal financial risk for the owner. Additionally, banks may be reluctant to lend to a small, relatively new food business with limited track record.')
bullet('[An] AGAINST: Alternative sources may be more appropriate. [An] Crowdfunding provides finance plus marketing; retained profits avoid debt entirely; trade credit from ingredient suppliers provides short-term flexibility without formal borrowing.')
bullet('[Ev] JUDGEMENT: A bank loan is appropriate for JC if: the expansion requires significant upfront investment (new premises), JC has sufficient trading history to satisfy the bank, and projected cash flows comfortably cover repayments. For smaller investments, retained profits or supplier credit may be preferable. For a completely new venture, crowdfunding might better serve both finance and marketing needs. The best source depends on the amount needed, JC track record, and available collateral.')
sp()

